window.UNF_REVIEW_CARDS={"t-1":{"title":"SMA","line":"The Simple Moving Average (SMA) compresses a recent stretch of prices into a single average line to help answer whether the broader backdrop is rising, falling, or moving sideways.","points":[["What you see","Price closes above the line repeatedly while the line slopes upward: Recent closes are generally above the average level, and the average level itself is rising."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the next bar is bound to rise, nor does it mean the entry price is cheap."],["Common mistake","Treating every close above the SMA as an automatic rally trigger and getting whipsawed repeatedly during sideways ranges."]]},"t-2":{"title":"EMA","line":"The Exponential Moving Average (EMA) also organizes prices into an average line, but assigns greater weight to the most recent closing prices, so it typically turns faster than an SMA of the same bar length.","points":[["What you see","The EMA moves up consecutively and most closes sit above the line: Recent prices are pulling the smoothed average upward."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every position is profitable, nor does it assure future gains."],["Common mistake","Assuming faster response always means higher accuracy when heavy weight on the latest bar causes more false turns in choppy markets."]]},"t-3":{"title":"WMA","line":"The Weighted Moving Average (WMA) averages the last N bars using uneven weights; the standard linear version gives the largest weight to the newest bar and progressively smaller weights to older bars.","points":[["What you see","The WMA sits above the same-period SMA and both slope upward: More recent prices are higher on average, so assigning them heavier weights lifts the WMA above the SMA."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the WMA has detected an unannounced future rally."],["Common mistake","Confusing linear time-weighted WMA with volume-weighted VWMA when WMA does not use volume at all."]]},"t-4":{"title":"SMMA/RMA","line":"The Smoothed Moving Average / Running Moving Average (SMMA / RMA) blends new prices into the prior average very gradually; a standard N-period RMA gives the newest price a weight of just 1/N.","points":[["What you see","Price spikes sharply, but the RMA ticks up only slightly: The new price carries only a small weight while the prior average still dominates."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the price rally was small or that the indicator is broken."],["Common mistake","Assuming a 14-bar SMMA/RMA has the same responsiveness as a 14-bar EMA when its 1/N weight makes it roughly twice as slow."]]},"t-5":{"title":"VWMA","line":"The Volume Weighted Moving Average (VWMA) weights the last N closing prices by each bar's trading volume: bars with heavier volume exert a larger pull on the average price.","points":[["What you see","The VWMA sits above the same-period SMA: Bars with heavier volume inside the window generally occurred at higher prices."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove institutions are net accumulating or that future prices will rise."],["Common mistake","Confusing a rolling N-bar VWMA with a session-anchored VWAP."]]},"t-6":{"title":"HMA","line":"The Hull Moving Average (HMA) combines several weighted moving averages to keep the line smooth while reducing lag as much as possible.","points":[["What you see","The HMA slopes upward consecutively while price forms higher lows: The smoothed direction aligns with recent improvement in actual prices."],["What it does not mean","There is no rule that the next pullback will hold above the line."],["Common mistake","Assuming low lag means zero false signals and trading every slope flip during sideways chop."]]},"t-7":{"title":"DEMA","line":"The Double Exponential Moving Average (DEMA) is plotted as a single composite line on the chart: 2 times the first EMA minus a doubly smoothed EMA (an EMA of the first EMA).","points":[["What you see","The DEMA slopes upward and sits above a standard EMA of the same period: Recent price gains push the lag-reduced composite higher and faster than a single EMA."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean DEMA sees extra market data invisible to other averages."],["Common mistake","Thinking 'Double EMA' means plotting two separate EMA lines for crossovers."]]},"t-8":{"title":"TEMA","line":"The Triple Exponential Moving Average (TEMA) combines three EMA layers into a single price line using 3×EMA1 − 3×EMA2 + EMA3 to further reduce moving-average lag. 'Triple' refers to the three smoothing layers in the formula, not three independent pieces of evidence.","points":[["What you see","The TEMA rises consecutively and closes mostly stay above the line: Recent prices are pulling the composite average upward in a relatively consistent direction."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove that the probability of further gains is locked in."],["Common mistake","Assuming 'Triple' means three times more reliable when TEMA is actually the fastest and most sensitive of the EMA/DEMA/TEMA family."]]},"t-9":{"title":"MA Cross","line":"A Moving Average Cross (MA Cross) plots a shorter-term average price alongside a longer-term average price to compare whether recent prices are stronger or weaker than the broader backdrop.","points":[["What you see","The short average crosses above the long average, and both lines subsequently slope upward: The near-term average has improved and is beginning to pull the longer average higher."],["What it does not mean","It does not pinpoint the exact start of a rally, nor does it rule out pullbacks after entry."],["Common mistake","Treating 'Golden Cross' or 'Death Cross' names as crystal-ball predictions when both lines are lagging summaries of past bars."]]},"t-10":{"title":"Moving Average Ribbon","line":"A Moving Average Ribbon plots a bundle of moving averages of progressive lengths on the same chart so you can see whether short-, medium-, and longer-term price directions align.","points":[["What you see","The 10, 20, 40, and 80 averages are stacked neatly from top to bottom and all slope upward: Shorter windows average higher prices than longer windows, and all lookback horizons temporarily point upward together."],["What it does not mean","Four aligned lines are not four independent forecasts, nor do they rule out a pullback."],["Common mistake","Treating four moving averages on the same chart as four independent sources of evidence when all four average the exact same price bars."]]},"t-11":{"title":"RSI","line":"The Relative Strength Index (RSI) converts the average size of recent price gains versus average losses into a score between 0 and 100.","points":[["What you see","RSI rises above 70 and remains elevated: Recent upward moves clearly outweigh downward moves on average, which often occurs during a strong trend."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean a 70% probability of rising or falling, nor does price have to pull back immediately."],["Common mistake","Treating the labels 'overbought' (70) and 'oversold' (30) as automatic sell and buy commands, which leads to shorting strong uptrends or catching falling knives in downtrends."]]},"t-12":{"title":"MACD","line":"MACD subtracts a slower EMA from a faster EMA to form the MACD line, then smooths that difference with a signal line; the histogram bars show the remaining gap between those two lines.","points":[["What you see","The MACD line sits above zero and positive histogram bars grow taller bar by bar: The fast average is above the slow average, and its lead over the signal line is widening."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every single price bar closes higher or that the next bar is bound to rise."],["Common mistake","Confusing a shrinking positive histogram (decelerating gains) with an actual price drop during a steady grind higher."]]},"t-13":{"title":"Stochastic/KD","line":"The Stochastic Oscillator (KD) measures where the closing price sits within the high-low range of a recent lookback window: closes near the top of the range produce high readings, while closes near the bottom produce low readings. %K is the faster line and %D is a moving average of %K.","points":[["What you see","Both %K and %D remain above 80: Smoothed closing locations are consistently near the top of the recent range, often seen in strong uptrends."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure an immediate drop, nor does it mean price rose by 80%."],["Common mistake","Shorting a strong uptrend simply because Stochastic crosses above 80, where closes naturally stay near the top of the rolling window."]]},"t-14":{"title":"Williams %R","line":"Williams %R also measures where the closing price sits within the recent high-low range, but plots it on an inverted 0 to −100 scale: readings near 0 sit near the top of the range, while readings near −100 sit near the bottom.","points":[["What you see","%R sits above −20, such as −10: The close is very close to the top of the recent range, showing strong relative position."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price has risen 90% or that you must sell immediately."],["Common mistake","Ignoring the minus sign and reading -80 as higher than -20, or failing to check whether a platform flips the axis to positive numbers."]]},"t-15":{"title":"CCI","line":"The Commodity Channel Index (CCI) measures how far a bar's typical price sits from its recent average, scaled by the average size of normal deviations over that window.","points":[["What you see","CCI climbs from 0 to +150 and stays elevated: Price is deviating upward from its recent average by more than usual, consistent with a strong push."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the market is overheated and about to reverse immediately."],["Common mistake","Assuming +100 and -100 are hard ceilings and shorting mechanically at +100 when strong breakouts can push CCI past +200."]]},"t-16":{"title":"ROC","line":"Rate of Change (ROC) directly answers 'by what percentage has price risen or fallen compared with N bars ago?' Converting price differences into percentages makes it easy to track how momentum over a fixed span is evolving.","points":[["What you see","ROC sits above 0 and continues rising: The percentage gain relative to the rolling anchor bar is expanding."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure the next bar will rise, nor does it mean every bar inside the window rose."],["Common mistake","Assuming a falling positive ROC means price is falling when price is actually still rising at a slower pace or against a higher prior base."]]},"t-17":{"title":"Momentum","line":"The standard difference version of Momentum (MTM) subtracts the close N bars ago from the current close, directly showing how many dollars or yuan price has moved over that span.","points":[["What you see","The difference is positive and increasing: Current price is higher than N bars ago, and the point gain across the window is widening."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove future bars will maintain the same pace."],["Common mistake","Failing to check whether a platform centers MTM at 0 or 100."]]},"t-18":{"title":"PPO","line":"The Percentage Price Oscillator (PPO) divides the difference between a fast EMA and a slow EMA by the slow EMA, expressing their spread as a percentage.","points":[["What you see","PPO is positive and rising: The percentage lead of the fast average over the slow average is widening."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every price bar rises, nor does it represent portfolio return."],["Common mistake","Assuming percentage normalization removes the lag or sideways whipsaws of 12- and 26-bar EMAs."]]},"t-19":{"title":"MFI","line":"The Money Flow Index (MFI) combines bar-to-bar changes in typical price with trading volume and converts them into a score between 0 and 100.","points":[["What you see","MFI stays above 80 for several bars: Up-typical-price bars carry a dominant share of recent volume-weighted flow."],["What it does not mean","You cannot assume an elevated reading must drop immediately, nor is it a true net cash inflow ratio."],["Common mistake","Assuming 'Money Flow' tracks actual net cash inflows when every trade has a buyer and a seller and the formula dumps 100% of a bar's volume into one bucket based on a tiny TP tick."]]},"t-20":{"title":"Bull/Bear Power","line":"Bull/Bear Power subtracts the same EMA from a bar's high and low to measure how far the two extremes of a candlestick reach from the recent average: Bull Power = High − EMA, and Bear Power = Low − EMA.","points":[["What you see","Bull Power is positive and rising while Bear Power moves from negative toward 0: Upper highs are reaching further above the average while lows are being pulled up toward the average."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove that the number of buyers is increasing."],["Common mistake","Failing to notice whether your charting platform plots Bull and Bear Power separately or adds them together as High + Low - 2×EMA."]]},"t-21":{"title":"Bollinger Bands","line":"Bollinger Bands plot a recent average price line in the middle, then draw upper and lower bands at a multiple of price standard deviation above and below it.","points":[["What you see","The bands narrow tightly while price moves within a small range: Recent price dispersion has shrunk, placing the market in a relatively quiet state."],["What it does not mean","It does not tell you whether the next move will be up or down, and quiet conditions can persist."],["Common mistake","Mechanically shorting every upper-band touch and buying every lower-band touch, which gets run over when volatility expands and price walks the band in a strong breakout."]]},"t-22":{"title":"Bollinger BandWidth","line":"Bollinger BandWidth (BBW) turns the distance between the upper and lower Bollinger Bands into a single number, commonly calculated as (Upper Band − Lower Band) ÷ Middle Band × 100.","points":[["What you see","BBW sits near the lowest levels of the asset's recent history: Recent prices are tightly clustered, reflecting a relatively quiet state."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure an immediate breakout, nor does it indicate which way price will break."],["Common mistake","Treating low BandWidth as a bullish signal when downward breakdowns expand BandWidth just as rapidly as rallies."]]},"t-23":{"title":"Bollinger %B","line":"Bollinger %B converts where price sits between the upper and lower Bollinger Bands into a coordinate: the lower band is 0, the middle band is 0.5, and the upper band is 1.","points":[["What you see","%B stays near 1 or above 1 consecutively: Price is hugging or closing outside the recent upper band, showing strong relative position."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove price will drop immediately or cannot climb further."],["Common mistake","Shorting blindly whenever %B exceeds 1.0, overlooking that strong breakouts frequently close outside the upper band as a new trend starts."]]},"t-24":{"title":"Donchian","line":"Donchian Channels plot the highest high and lowest low over the last N bars as the upper and lower bands, with the midpoint between those two extremes as the middle line.","points":[["What you see","Both the upper and lower bands step higher over time: The recent high-low trading range is shifting upward."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that prices will keep rising indefinitely."],["Common mistake","Waiting for a candle to trade 'above' a current-bar Donchian upper band that includes the current bar's high (which is mathematically impossible—you must compare against the prior bar's band)."]]},"t-25":{"title":"Keltner","line":"Modern Keltner Channels typically use an EMA as the middle line and set the upper and lower bands at a multiple of Average True Range (ATR) above and below that EMA.","points":[["What you see","The middle line slopes upward while closes consistently stay in the upper half or near the upper band: The average direction is rising and recent prices are consistently stronger than the average."],["What it does not mean","Touching the upper band does not automatically mean you should sell or that price has topped."],["Common mistake","Failing to check whether a platform uses the modern EMA ± ATR formula or Keltner's 1960 SMA ± High-Low range formula."]]},"t-26":{"title":"Moving Average Envelopes","line":"Moving Average Envelopes plot a moving average as the center line and shift it up and down by a fixed percentage to form upper and lower bands.","points":[["What you see","Price stays mostly in the upper half of the envelope while all three lines slope upward: Price is strong relative to its average, and the average backdrop itself is rising."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that price will pull back upon touching the upper band."],["Common mistake","Using a single textbook percentage like 3% across low-volatility indices and high-volatility crypto alike."]]},"t-27":{"title":"ATR","line":"Average True Range (ATR) measures how much a single candlestick typically spans over a recent window, including both the bar's high-low range and any gap from the prior bar's close.","points":[["What you see","ATR rises noticeably above its own recent levels: Typical single-bar price movement has expanded, so tight price distances are more easily swept through."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is trending upward, nor does it signal an imminent reversal."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a rising ATR line for a bullish price trend when panic sell-offs frequently cause the steepest ATR spikes."]]},"t-28":{"title":"ATRP/NATR","line":"Average True Range Percent / Normalized ATR (ATRP / NATR) typically divides ATR by the closing price and multiplies by 100, answering 'what percentage of current price does a typical single-bar move represent?' Converting raw price units into percentages places high-priced and low-priced assets on a comparable relative volatility scale.","points":[["What you see","ATRP rises on a given asset: Average true range now represents a larger percentage of the current closing price."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is rising; in fact, a falling price shrinks the denominator and can push ATRP up."],["Common mistake","Mixing scripts that compute ATR(14)/Close with scripts that compute EMA(TR/Close, 14) without checking the code."]]},"t-29":{"title":"ADR","line":"Average Daily Range (ADR) calculates (High − Low) for each of the past N days and averages those daily high-low spans, describing 'how far price typically travels between the day's high and low within a single session.' Because it usually excludes overnight gaps from yesterday's close to today's open, it differs from ATR.","points":[["What you see","Today's high-low range so far is much smaller than the ADR: Intraday movement up to this point is smaller than the recent daily average—either because the session is still early or because trading is quiet."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is obligated to travel the remaining distance before the close."],["Common mistake","Treating an average (1x ADR) as a daily maximum limit and fading a strong trend day just because price already traveled 1x ADR from the morning low."]]},"t-30":{"title":"Standard Deviation","line":"Standard Deviation condenses how widely a recent set of prices is dispersed around its own average into a single number: readings are small when prices cluster tightly near the mean and large when prices spread far apart.","points":[["What you see","Standard Deviation is rising: Values inside the window are more spread out around their mean—either from wider swings or a strong trend stretching prices apart."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is moving upward."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a rising Standard Deviation curve for a bullish trend when a sharp crash spikes squared deviations just as much as a rally."]]},"t-31":{"title":"Historical Volatility","line":"Historical Volatility (HV) measures how unstable bar-to-bar percentage returns have been over a recent lookback window, usually scaled into an annualized percentage.","points":[["What you see","HV climbs from a low level: Bar-to-bar return variations inside the lookback window have grown wider, indicating higher realized volatility."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is rising—a sharp sell-off also drives HV up."],["Common mistake","Mistaking 40% Historical Volatility for a 40% return forecast or a hard cap on future losses."]]},"t-32":{"title":"Volume","line":"Trading Volume records how many shares, coins, or contracts changed hands inside a single bar, while a Volume Moving Average (Volume MA) shows the typical level over the last N bars.","points":[["What you see","Price rises on volume noticeably above the Volume MA: The advance was accompanied by heavier-than-usual turnover and active participation."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove buyers won permanently or that the next bar is bound to rise."],["Common mistake","Thinking a tall green volume bar means buyers outnumbered sellers when every traded share has both a buyer and a seller and bar color only reflects Close versus Open."]]},"t-33":{"title":"RVOL","line":"Relative Volume (RVOL) divides current volume by normal benchmark volume to answer 'how many times busier than usual is trading right now?' A reading of 1 matches the benchmark; 2 means twice the benchmark.","points":[["What you see","RVOL is above 1, such as 2.0: Current volume is roughly twice the chosen benchmark, showing elevated activity."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is rising—panic selling also produces high RVOL."],["Common mistake","Using a simple rolling N-bar RVOL on intraday charts and getting tricked by the normal opening-bell volume jump every day."]]},"t-34":{"title":"OBV","line":"On-Balance Volume (OBV) keeps a running score of volume based on bar-to-bar closing direction: if a bar closes higher than the prior close, its entire volume is added; if it closes lower, its entire volume is subtracted; if unchanged, OBV stays flat.","points":[["What you see","Both price and OBV reach new recent highs: Price improvement and the cumulative balance of up-close volume are aligned."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that the next breakout attempt will succeed."],["Common mistake","Reading the raw cumulative number on the axis as dollar inflow when the number changes completely depending on the chart's start date."]]},"t-35":{"title":"PVT","line":"Price Volume Trend (PVT) multiplies each bar's percentage close change by that bar's volume and adds the result to a running cumulative total.","points":[["What you see","Price trends higher and PVT also climbs: Percentage gains multiplied by volume are producing positive cumulative additions, keeping volume and price aligned."],["What it does not mean","It cannot confirm specific institutional buying or assure future gains."],["Common mistake","Assuming PVT measures actual institutional cash inflows when it simply multiplies percentage price change by total volume."]]},"t-36":{"title":"PVO","line":"The Percentage Volume Oscillator (PVO) measures by what percentage a short-term EMA of volume sits above or below a longer-term EMA of volume.","points":[["What you see","PVO sits above 0 and rises: Short-term average volume exceeds long-term average volume, and that activity lead is widening."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure rising prices—panic sell-offs can also drive PVO sharply higher."],["Common mistake","Treating PVO like price MACD and buying every bullish cross or zero cross, forgetting that heavy panic selling also pushes short-term volume above long-term volume."]]},"t-37":{"title":"VWAP","line":"Volume Weighted Average Price (VWAP) accumulates (Price × Volume) from a chosen anchor start point and divides by cumulative volume from that same start point, producing the volume-weighted average price of the session.","points":[["What you see","Price holds above VWAP while the VWAP line slopes upward: Current trades are clearing above the session's cumulative volume center, pulling the session average higher."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure VWAP will hold as support on the next dip or that the next bar will rise."],["Common mistake","Loading a Session-anchored VWAP on a daily (1D) chart where it resets every single bar and merely traces each bar's typical price."]]},"t-38":{"title":"Parabolic SAR","line":"Parabolic SAR plots a series of dots either below or above price bars, following a step-up acceleration formula to trail the algorithm's current directional state.","points":[["What you see","Dots stay below price and step higher on each bar: The algorithm remains in its upward trailing state and is ratcheting its reference level higher."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure price cannot gap through or pierce the dot on the next bar."],["Common mistake","Mechanically stopping and reversing on every dot flip inside a sideways range, resulting in constant whipsaw losses."]]},"t-39":{"title":"Supertrend","line":"Supertrend uses Average True Range (ATR) and a multiplier to plot a trailing reference line on one side of price, flipping to the opposite side when price crosses the line.","points":[["What you see","The line sits below price and steps higher while closes hold above it: The algorithm remains in its upward state as the ratcheted boundary moves up with price."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure price will bounce off the line when tested."],["Common mistake","Being fooled by the word 'Super' into trading every color flip during sideways chop, where the 3×ATR lag buys near the top of the range and sells near the bottom."]]},"t-40":{"title":"Price Relative","line":"Price Relative (Relative Strength Ratio) divides Asset A's price by Asset B's price (A / B) to track whether the ratio is rising or falling over time.","points":[["What you see","Asset A rises 10% while Asset B rises 5%, pushing the A/B ratio higher: Asset A outperformed Asset B over this period while both rose."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove Asset A will keep leading in the future or is fundamentally cheaper."],["Common mistake","Confusing relative outperformance with positive dollar returns and buying an asset with a rising ratio during a bear market where it is still falling in price."]]},"t-41":{"title":"ADX","line":"First answer whether the market has been moving persistently in one direction before deciding whether a trend is worth studying further.","points":[["What you see","ADX climbs from 15 to 28: Over the lookback window, one directional side has increasingly dominated; look back at the main chart to see whether that side is up or down."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price rose 28%, nor does it mean buyers are bound to win."],["Common mistake","Buying simply because ADX is high, forgetting that sharp sell-offs also push ADX up and risking losses against a downtrend."]]},"t-42":{"title":"DMI","line":"Separate market action into two distinct questions—direction and strength: +DI and −DI compare upward push versus downward pressure, while ADX checks whether that lead is persisting.","points":[["What you see","+DI sits above −DI while ADX is rising: Upward directional movement leads and trend consistency is strengthening; check the main chart for higher highs and higher lows."],["What it does not mean","It is not a 'golden cross' that guarantees further price gains."],["Common mistake","Trading every +DI/-DI crossover while ADX is flat and low, resulting in repeated whipsaw losses during sideways markets."]]},"t-43":{"title":"Aroon","line":"Measure how recently the highest high and lowest low inside a lookback window occurred.","points":[["What you see","Aroon Up sits near the top while Aroon Down sits near the bottom: A window high occurred recently while the window low happened long ago, tilting recent structure upward."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the probability of rising equals the Aroon Up number."],["Common mistake","Assuming Aroon Up at 100 means a massive rally when a tiny new high produces the exact same 100 reading."]]},"t-44":{"title":"Aroon Oscillator","line":"Compress the lead between the two Aroon lines into a single curve: Aroon Oscillator = Aroon Up − Aroon Down.","points":[["What you see","The curve crosses from negative to positive above the zero line: The window's highest high is now more recent than its lowest low."],["What it does not mean","It is not price crossing a cost basis line, nor does it assure a sustained rally."],["Common mistake","Shorting simply because the oscillator nears +100, overlooking that steady uptrends keep highs fresh for extended periods."]]},"t-45":{"title":"Vortex","line":"Compare upward versus downward bar-to-bar movement across adjacent candlesticks: the Vortex Indicator plots VI+ (from current highs minus prior lows) and VI− (from current lows minus prior highs), both scaled by True Range, to highlight shifts in directional structure.","points":[["What you see","VI+ crosses above VI− from below and holds its lead: Upward cross-bar movement now outweighs downward movement over the window, making upside structure worth watching."],["What it does not mean","Not every crossover is an automatic buy signal."],["Common mistake","Reacting to every crossover during tight sideways trading where overlapping bars generate constant whipsaws."]]},"t-46":{"title":"Choppiness Index","line":"Distinguish whether price has been grinding in circles inside a box or traveling smoothly in one net direction.","points":[["What you see","CHOP stays elevated above 61.8: The lookback window is overlapping and choppy, where trend-chasing entries easily get whipsawed."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean individual bars have tiny high-low ranges."],["Common mistake","Buying solely because CHOP drops below 38.2, forgetting that a steep decline also produces low CHOP readings."]]},"t-47":{"title":"Ichimoku","line":"View near-term equilibrium, medium-term equilibrium, and a broader support/resistance zone on a single chart.","points":[["What you see","Candlesticks hold above the Cloud: Price sits in a strong position relative to this set of historical midpoint zones; check whether the Base Line and actual swing highs/lows are also rising."],["What it does not mean","Sharp sell-offs can still start while price is above the Cloud."],["Common mistake","Treating the forward-shifted cloud on the right of the chart as a future forecast when it is purely historical midpoints shifted forward."]]},"t-48":{"title":"Williams Alligator","line":"Use three smoothed moving averages of different speeds—shifted slightly forward—to see whether price action has transitioned from tangled congestion into an orderly directional run.","points":[["What you see","All three lines are braided tightly together as price crosses back and forth: Reference averages across the three speeds have not separated, indicating weak directional order ('sleeping alligator')."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee an immediate breakout is about to happen."],["Common mistake","Chasing every minor separation during sideways chop where lag causes the lines to open right as the short swing ends."]]},"t-49":{"title":"KAMA","line":"Observe an adaptive moving average that changes its smoothing speed based on how directional or choppy recent price action has been.","points":[["What you see","KAMA slopes upward and most closes sit above it: The adaptively smoothed direction points upward."],["What it does not mean","The line is not an unbreakable floor that price cannot fall through."],["Common mistake","Treating 'adaptive' as magic zero-lag prediction and expecting instant turns during sharp V-reversals."]]},"t-50":{"title":"ALMA","line":"Use a Gaussian bell-curve weighted price average to read trend direction while reducing single-bar jumpiness.","points":[["What you see","The line slopes upward consecutively and most closes sit above it: The Gaussian-weighted recent price reference is rising and current price sits above it."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that every pullback to the curve will bounce."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a visually smooth curve for predictive accuracy and reacting late when turns lag behind sharp reversals."]]},"t-51":{"title":"McGinley Dynamic","line":"Use a dynamic line whose tracking speed adjusts to its ratio with current price to read overall direction and deviation.","points":[["What you see","The curve slopes upward while consecutive closes stay above it: The smoothed historical direction and current closing position are both aligned upward."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure the line will hold as support on the next pullback."],["Common mistake","Assuming 'reduced lag' means zero lag and expecting instant signals at market tops or bottoms."]]},"t-52":{"title":"LSMA","line":"See where the right-hand endpoint of a best-fit straight line through recent prices lands today.","points":[["What you see","The LSMA slopes upward consecutively: The local best-fit direction points upward; a steeper slope reflects faster recent linear progression under this setting."],["What it does not mean","Future prices are not obligated to keep following that fitted line."],["Common mistake","Confusing a best-fit line of past bars with a forecast of future bars."]]},"t-53":{"title":"Linear Regression Channel","line":"Separate the overall straight-line direction of recent prices from how far current price sits to either side of that centerline.","points":[["What you see","The channel slopes upward and price trades near the upper boundary: Price sits on the strong upper side of an upward-sloping recent trajectory."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure an immediate drop back to the middle line."],["Common mistake","Forgetting that a rolling regression channel recalculates and repaints its historical angle as new bars arrive, making past charts look deceptively tidy."]]},"t-54":{"title":"Linear Regression Slope","line":"Quantify the upward or downward tilt of a best-fit straight line through recent prices to see whether directional speed is changing.","points":[["What you see","Raw slope climbs from +0.5 to +0.9: Under the same window and timeframe, the upward linear progression has steepened."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean tomorrow's bar is guaranteed to rise by 0.9 units."],["Common mistake","Comparing raw unnormalized slope values across assets with different price levels."]]},"t-55":{"title":"Linear Regression R²","line":"Check how well a straight line actually summarizes recent prices.","points":[["What you see","R² sits near 0.90: Recent prices follow a nearly straight-line path, so linear regression explains a large share of the window's variance."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean a 90% win rate on future price predictions."],["Common mistake","Mistaking R² for the probability of a rally or model win rate when a steep sell-off also scores near 1.0."]]},"t-56":{"title":"Correlation Coefficient","line":"Measure whether two series have tended to move up and down together over a recent lookback window.","points":[["What you see","The coefficient sits near +0.90: Over this window, the two series show a strong positive linear relationship (tending to sit above or below their means together)."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean a 1% gain in Asset A forces an exact 0.9% gain in Asset B."],["Common mistake","Treating correlation as causation and trading Asset B blindly off Asset A's ticks."]]},"t-57":{"title":"StochRSI","line":"Measure where the RSI momentum oscillator currently sits within its own recent high-low range.","points":[["What you see","Both lines sit near 100 (or 1.0): RSI is near the top of its own recent lookback range, showing strong short-term relative momentum."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price will drop immediately or that price rose 100%."],["Common mistake","Treating 80 and 20 as automatic reversal triggers when StochRSI is hyper-sensitive and hits extremes even during mild sideways drift."]]},"t-58":{"title":"ConnorsRSI","line":"Combine short-term RSI momentum, the duration of consecutive up or down closes (streak length), and the percentile rank of today's single-bar percentage return into one composite score to spot short-term price extension.","points":[["What you see","The reading approaches 90: All three short-term dimensions combined sit near the top of their scales, indicating a stretched short-term upward run."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean a 90% probability that price will fall tomorrow."],["Common mistake","Applying standard 70/30 RSI levels to CRSI, which swings much wider due to its short 3-bar and 2-bar inputs."]]},"t-59":{"title":"CMO","line":"Compare total upward point gains against total downward point losses over a lookback window to see which side accumulated more ground.","points":[["What you see","CMO stays in positive territory and climbs higher: Cumulative upward point gains over the window are expanding their lead over downward losses."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every single candlestick inside the window closed higher."],["Common mistake","Automatically shorting at +50 or buying at -50 when strong trends can keep gains dominating losses for many bars."]]},"t-60":{"title":"True Strength Index","line":"Double-smooth bar-to-bar price changes and divide by double-smoothed absolute price changes to see whether net momentum points up or down.","points":[["What you see","TSI sits above the zero line and slopes upward: Double-smoothed net upward price changes dominate and are strengthening."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean future percentage price gains will equal the TSI number."],["Common mistake","Overlooking the lag introduced by two layers of smoothing and entering late after a short swing has already exhausted itself."]]},"t-61":{"title":"TRIX","line":"See whether the 1-bar percentage rate of change of a triple-smoothed EMA is positive or negative.","points":[["What you see","TRIX is positive and rising: The triple-smoothed EMA is still rising, and its bar-to-bar percentage growth rate is accelerating."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that every raw price candlestick closes higher."],["Common mistake","Confusing a smooth, quiet indicator line with low risk when triple smoothing delays turn signals during fast sell-offs."]]},"t-62":{"title":"Ultimate Oscillator","line":"Blend price-push ratios across short, medium, and long lookback windows (typically 7, 14, and 28 bars) into a single score to reduce the blind spots of any single period length.","points":[["What you see","The reading sits above 70: Multi-window closes have consistently finished near the upper end of their true ranges, placing momentum in an elevated zone."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price must drop immediately."],["Common mistake","Treating the name 'Ultimate' as proof of infallibility."]]},"t-63":{"title":"Awesome Oscillator/AO","line":"Compare a short-term simple moving average of bar midpoints against a longer-term simple moving average of bar midpoints to see whether near-term midpoint momentum leads or lags the broader backdrop.","points":[["What you see","Histogram bars sit above zero and grow taller: The 5-bar midpoint average sits above the 34-bar average, and its lead is widening."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every single candlestick closed higher."],["Common mistake","Confusing green/red bar colors with positive/negative values and buying a green bar deep below zero thinking the trend has already turned positive."]]},"t-64":{"title":"SMI","line":"Measure whether the closing price sits in the upper half or lower half of its recent high-low range, then double-smooth that distance relative to the double-smoothed range width.","points":[["What you see","SMI sits clearly in positive territory: Double-smoothed closes are positioned in the upper half of the recent high-low range."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price rose by that same percentage."],["Common mistake","Confusing Stochastic Momentum Index with SMI Ergodic or StochRSI when searching indicators."]]},"t-65":{"title":"SMI Ergodic","line":"Compare double-smoothed net price changes against double-smoothed absolute price changes (matching the True Strength Index formula) and pair that main line with a slower signal EMA to see whether directional momentum is improving.","points":[["What you see","The main line is positive and sits above the signal line: Net upward momentum dominates and is stronger than its slower signal reference."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee that prices will keep rising."],["Common mistake","Confusing SMI Ergodic with the midpoint-based Stochastic Momentum Index and applying ±40 levels to a -1 to +1 scale."]]},"t-66":{"title":"Relative Vigor Index","line":"Compare a bar's net intrabar progress (Close − Open) against its total intrabar range (High − Low) to see whether bars have been consistently finishing near their highs or lows relative to where they opened.","points":[["What you see","The RVI main line stays positive: Over recent bars, closes have generally finished above opens relative to bar ranges."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee today's close is higher than yesterday's close, since overnight gaps between yesterday's close and today's open are not inside (Close − Open)."],["Common mistake","Typing 'RVI' into search and accidentally loading the Relative Volatility Index."]]},"t-67":{"title":"Relative Volatility Index","line":"Compare price volatility on up-close bars against price volatility on down-close bars to see which directional side carries greater volatility weight on a 0 to 100 scale.","points":[["What you see","The reading holds consistently above 50: Under this formula, up-close bars account for a larger share of recent smoothed price standard deviation."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the probability of future gains is above 50%."],["Common mistake","Mistaking RVI for a directionless volatility gauge like ATR when it actually measures directional bias between up-bar and down-bar variability."]]},"t-68":{"title":"RCI","line":"Measure how closely the rank order of closing prices (from lowest to highest) matches the chronological order of time (from earliest to latest) inside a lookback window.","points":[["What you see","RCI sits near +100: More recent closes consistently rank higher than earlier closes inside the window, showing a very orderly upward sequence."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price has doubled (+100%)."],["Common mistake","Assuming RCI near +100 means a huge price gain when tiny consecutive ticks also score +100 and can be wiped out by one large down bar."]]},"t-69":{"title":"Fisher Transform","line":"Apply the inverse hyperbolic tangent (Fisher Transform) to a normalized range-location series so that readings near the outer edges of a recent high-low window are stretched out into sharp, clearly visible peaks and troughs.","points":[["What you see","Fisher climbs to an elevated positive reading: Price has been hugging the upper edge of its recent lookback range, pushing the transformed score to a local extreme."],["What it does not mean","An elevated reading does not assure the next bar will fall."],["Common mistake","Applying a rigid textbook threshold like ±1.5 across all instruments when Fisher values are unbounded and vary by implementation."]]},"t-70":{"title":"DPO","line":"Strip out a longer-term moving average trend so you can examine shorter-term high-and-low cycles around that baseline.","points":[["What you see","DPO forms a peak above the zero line: At that historical bar, price sat above the detrended moving-average baseline, marking a local cycle high."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure current live price is topping right now."],["Common mistake","Being fooled by Centered mode's hindsight alignment on past bars when that calculation uses 11 future bars that did not exist at the time."]]},"t-71":{"title":"KST","line":"Combine smoothed rates of change (ROC) across four different lookback lengths into a single momentum curve to reduce the blind spots of watching only one timeframe window.","points":[["What you see","KST is positive and sits above its signal line: Composite multi-window rate of change tilts upward and is currently stronger than its slower signal reference."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that all four underlying ROC spans are individually positive."],["Common mistake","Taking the name 'Know Sure Thing' literally as a ensure when no indicator can predict the future with certainty."]]},"t-72":{"title":"Pring Special K","line":"Aggregate smoothed rates of change across many short, intermediate, and long lookback windows into a single curve to observe the broader multi-horizon momentum backdrop.","points":[["What you see","The main line trends upward and sits above its slow signal line: Momentum combined across multiple time horizons is improving."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every short-term sub-window is rising simultaneously."],["Common mistake","Loading Special K on newly listed instruments with fewer than 725 bars and wondering why nothing plots."]]},"t-73":{"title":"PMO","line":"Observe 1-bar percentage price changes after two layers of custom exponential smoothing to see whether smoothed relative momentum is positive, negative, or slowing down.","points":[["What you see","PMO is positive and rising: Double-smoothed upward percentage changes dominate and are strengthening."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure that every single price bar closes higher."],["Common mistake","Confusing a slower rate of decline with an actual uptrend and buying aggressively while PMO is still negative and price is drifting down."]]},"t-74":{"title":"Coppock Curve","line":"Monitor whether long-horizon momentum is beginning to turn up after a deep, prolonged downturn.","points":[["What you see","The curve forms a trough below zero and hooks upward: Long-horizon downward velocity is starting to ease—the classic 'momentum thaw' the indicator was built to spot."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee the exact lowest price is already behind you."],["Common mistake","Applying a formula designed specifically for 11- and 14-month horizons onto 1-minute charts and expecting the same reliability."]]},"t-75":{"title":"MACD-V","line":"Normalize the spread between a fast EMA and a slow EMA by dividing by Average True Range (ATR) so you can measure momentum in units of typical bar volatility.","points":[["What you see","The main line reads +100: The fast EMA sits above the slow EMA by a distance equal to 1.0× the 26-period ATR."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price has risen 100% or is about to double."],["Common mistake","Mistaking 'V' for Volume and loading a volume-weighted MACD instead of volatility-normalized MACD."]]},"t-76":{"title":"Mass Index","line":"Track whether the high-to-low range of candlesticks is widening sharply and then starting to contract.","points":[["What you see","The Mass Index crosses above 27: Under standard 9/25 settings, single-bar high-low ranges have expanded noticeably relative to their smoothed baseline."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price is moving upward—a steep sell-off also pushes Mass Index above 27."],["Common mistake","Taking a trade solely on a 27-to-26.5 bulge without checking price direction, forgetting that (High - Low) contains no up-or-down sign."]]},"t-77":{"title":"Accumulation/Distribution","line":"Track whether volume has been consistently accompanying closes near the top or bottom of each bar's high-low range, accumulating those weighted volumes over time.","points":[["What you see","The ADL climbs steadily over multiple bars: Bars closing in the upper half of their high-low ranges have contributed more volume weight than bars closing in the lower half."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove that net cash is flowing into the asset by that exact amount."],["Common mistake","Treating ADL as direct proof of institutional buying or selling when it only measures where price closed inside each bar's high-low range."]]},"t-78":{"title":"CMF","line":"Divide the sum of close-location-weighted volume over a rolling N-bar window by total volume over that same window to see whether volume has tilted toward upper-range or lower-range closes.","points":[["What you see","CMF holds near +0.20: Over the rolling window, net close-location-weighted volume is positive and equals 20% of total window volume."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean 20% of market capitalization flowed in as new cash."],["Common mistake","Assuming 'Money Flow' measures actual net cash entering the asset when every trade has both a buyer and a seller."]]},"t-79":{"title":"Chaikin Oscillator","line":"Measure the momentum of the Accumulation/Distribution Line (ADL) by subtracting a slower EMA of ADL from a faster EMA of ADL, rather than looking only at the cumulative ADL level.","points":[["What you see","The reading is positive and rising: The 3-period EMA of ADL sits above the 10-period EMA of ADL and the lead is widening, showing accelerating upper-range volume additions."],["What it does not mean","It does not represent net dollar inflows of that amount."],["Common mistake","Stacking ADL, CMF, and the Chaikin Oscillator on the same chart and treating their agreement as three separate confirmations when all three share the same formula inputs."]]},"t-80":{"title":"Klinger Oscillator","line":"Combine trading volume with typical-price direction and high-low range changes into a 'Volume Force' series, then subtract a slower EMA from a faster EMA of that series to track the rhythm of volume-price push.","points":[["What you see","The main line sits above zero and above its signal line: The faster Volume Force average leads the slower average, and near-term momentum also exceeds its signal reference."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee that subsequent price bars will rise."],["Common mistake","Treating 'Volume Force' as institutional account data when it is purely a bar-and-volume formula that varies across exchange feeds."]]},"t-81":{"title":"Elder Force Index","line":"Combine the size of a bar-to-bar closing price change with the trading volume on that bar: the larger the price change and the heavier the volume, the larger the Force Index reading.","points":[["What you see","Positive readings expand higher: Under this smoothing setting, up-close price changes multiplied by volume are growing stronger."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove every executed trade was an aggressive buyer."],["Common mistake","Comparing raw EFI numbers across symbols with different share prices or volume scales."]]},"t-82":{"title":"EOM/EMV","line":"Compare how far a bar's high-low midpoint moved against the bar's high-low range and trading volume to see whether price moved a given distance on relatively light or heavy volume.","points":[["What you see","Large positive readings: Under this formula, the high-low midpoint moved upward noticeably across wide ranges on relatively modest volume."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure deep order-book liquidity—thin markets also jump easily on low volume."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a low-volume price jump for safe strength when thin liquidity makes it equally easy for price to drop on small sell orders."]]},"t-83":{"title":"PVI","line":"Isolate and accumulate price performance strictly on bars where trading volume exceeded the prior bar's volume.","points":[["What you see","Volume expands and price closes higher, lifting PVI: The expanding-volume bar contributed a positive percentage return to the index."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every market participant made money."],["Common mistake","Believing textbook folklore that PVI isolates uninformed retail traders when institutional breakouts and index rebalancing also drive high-volume days."]]},"t-84":{"title":"NVI","line":"Isolate and accumulate price performance strictly on bars where trading volume was lower than the prior bar's volume.","points":[["What you see","Volume shrinks and price closes higher, lifting NVI: The lower-volume bar contributed a positive return to the conditional index."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove institutions are secretly accumulating."],["Common mistake","Misinterpreting 'Negative Volume' as net selling volume."]]},"t-85":{"title":"BOP","line":"Measure what fraction of a bar's total high-to-low range was retained as net open-to-close progress: Balance of Power (BOP) = (Close − Open) ÷ (High − Low).","points":[["What you see","BOP sits near +1.0: The bar opened near its low and closed near its high, retaining almost the entire intraday range as upward open-to-close progress."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean 100% of volume had only buyers."],["Common mistake","Treating a +1.0 reading as proof of heavy buying when a tiny 1-cent bar with no wicks also scores +1.0 and the formula excludes volume."]]},"t-86":{"title":"Relative Volume at Time","line":"Relative Volume at Time answers 'how busy is trading right now compared with the exact same clock time across prior sessions?' Volume counts how many shares, coins, or contracts traded—not percentage price gain or participant headcount.","points":[["What you see","The reading sits well above 1.0: Turnover during this time slot is noticeably heavier than the historical average for the same clock time."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean buyers outnumber sellers or that price is bound to rise—every trade matches a buyer and a seller."],["Common mistake","Buying blindly on a 2x or 3x volume spike when heavy panic selling also produces high relative volume."]]},"t-87":{"title":"TTM Squeeze","line":"TTM Squeeze separates two distinct questions: first, whether price volatility is compressed tighter than usual, and second, which way momentum is leaning once that compression releases.","points":[["What you see","Consecutive 'Squeeze On' dots print along the zero line: Under the chosen channel settings, price volatility is compressed inside a tight range."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee an immediate explosion, nor do the dots reveal whether the break will be up or down."],["Common mistake","Buying as soon as squeeze dots appear, forgetting that a squeeze only measures quiet compression and can fire sharply downward."]]},"t-88":{"title":"Ulcer Index","line":"The Ulcer Index specifically measures the depth and duration of percentage drawdowns from recent rolling peak prices.","points":[["What you see","The Ulcer Index climbs steadily from a low reading: Recent pullbacks from the window peak have grown deeper or lasted for more bars."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the next bar is bound to fall or give a bottom date."],["Common mistake","Assuming a falling Ulcer Index always means price is rallying when UI also drops simply because an old peak from 15 bars ago rolled out of the lookback window while price stayed flat at the bottom."]]},"t-89":{"title":"Chandelier Exit","line":"Chandelier Exit helps a trader who already holds a position frame the question: 'how far below the recent peak (or above the recent trough) can price pull back before I re-examine my thesis?' The standard long exit line subtracts a multiple of Average True Range (ATR—typical single-bar range) from the highest high of the lookback window, leaving a volatility-scaled buffer rather than estimating fair fundamental value.","points":[["What you see","Price holds above the Long Exit line and prints fresh highs: Under this trailing reference, the pullback threshold has not been breached."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean this is a low-risk fresh entry point or guarantee another new high."],["Common mistake","Assuming a visual indicator line on the chart automatically places or executes a protective stop order with your broker."]]},"t-90":{"title":"Chande Kroll Stop","line":"Chande Kroll Stop combines recent high-low price extremes and Average True Range (ATR) into two smoothed trailing exit reference lines (Stop Long and Stop Short).","points":[["What you see","Price closes above both Stop Long and Stop Short: Current price sits on the strong side of both trailing references, consistent with maintaining a long watch."],["What it does not mean","It is not an automatic buy command, nor does it mean price cannot drop through both lines."],["Common mistake","Trading every touch or cross while price chops inside the corridor between the two lines."]]},"t-91":{"title":"ATR Trailing Stop","line":"ATR Trailing Stop uses recent typical bar volatility to leave breathing room for an open position and ratchets the exit reference in the favorable direction as price advances.","points":[["What you see","During an advance, the protective line below price steps higher: This version is ratcheting its trailing exit reference up toward higher prices."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee the line will hold as support, nor does every upward step require adding to your position."],["Common mistake","Assuming all ATR Trailing Stop scripts share one formula when some anchor to Close and others to High/Low."]]},"t-92":{"title":"Volatility Stop","line":"Volatility Stop is a family of trailing exit methods that leave a wider buffer when recent price swings are large and a tighter buffer when recent swings are small.","points":[["What you see","Price trends in one direction while the Volatility Stop line trails behind it: The historical move has stayed within the volatility buffer without triggering a state flip."],["What it does not mean","It does not assure the trend will continue tomorrow or replace having an actual exit plan."],["Common mistake","Widening your stop distance on a volatile asset without scaling down your position size, which multiplies your dollar loss when stopped out."]]},"t-93":{"title":"Anchored VWAP","line":"Anchored VWAP (AVWAP) calculates the volume-weighted average price starting from a specific bar or event date that you choose.","points":[["What you see","Price closes consecutively above the Anchored VWAP while the line slopes upward: The cumulative volume-weighted average since the anchor event is rising and current price holds above it."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove every buyer since the anchor is in profit, nor does it guarantee a bounce on the next touch."],["Common mistake","Dragging the anchor candle across random bars in hindsight until the line magically lines up with a bounce."]]},"t-94":{"title":"Pivot Points","line":"Pivot Points Standard uses the completed high, low, and close of the prior period (such as yesterday, last week, or last month) to plot a set of horizontal reference lines across the current period: P is the central pivot, R1/R2/etc. are upper formula levels, and S1/S2/etc. are lower formula levels.","points":[["What you see","Price trades above the central Pivot (P): Current price sits in the upper half of today's prior-period formula map."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee today will close higher or mean you must buy every dip to P."],["Common mistake","Assuming 'R1' means the exact same price across Traditional, Fibonacci, and Camarilla formulas."]]},"t-95":{"title":"Pivot Points High/Low","line":"Pivot Points High/Low labels local price peaks and valleys across a chart.","points":[["What you see","Confirmed Pivot Highs and Confirmed Pivot Lows both step higher over time: Under this left/right filter scale, historical swing structure is trending upward."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee the next swing low will also hold higher, nor does it mean smaller timeframes are trending up."],["Common mistake","Looking at a static historical chart where every top and bottom has a neat label and thinking the indicator predicted those turns in real time, forgetting that each label was plotted R bars after the turn."]]},"t-96":{"title":"Fibonacci","line":"Fibonacci Retracements and Extensions divide a completed price swing into standard ratios, helping you answer how much of a prior move price has retraced or how far an extension projects relative to that move.","points":[["What you see","A pullback slows near 0.382 or 0.618 and candle bodies stop making lower lows: The retracement is pausing near a common ratio mark while staying inside the prior swing."],["What it does not mean","It does not make 0.618 an unbreakable floor or promise a new high."],["Common mistake","Placing blind limit orders at 0.382, 0.50, and 0.618 without waiting for price stabilization."]]},"t-97":{"title":"Williams Fractals","line":"Williams Fractals mark local five-bar (or N-bar) peaks and valleys on a price chart.","points":[["What you see","Successive confirmed high fractals and low fractals step higher: Local 5-bar turning points are stepping upward in a stair-step structure."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every down-fractal arrow was a profitable real-time entry."],["Common mistake","Assuming a fractal arrow on a historical peak was visible on that peak bar rather than 2 bars later."]]},"t-98":{"title":"ZigZag","line":"ZigZag filters out minor price fluctuations smaller than a chosen threshold and connects significant swing highs and swing lows with straight lines, letting you see the broad skeleton of historical price swings.","points":[["What you see","A small bounce occurs on the candlesticks without creating a new ZigZag leg: The bounce did not reach the minimum percentage Deviation or Depth threshold, so the filter ignored it."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the bounce did not happen on the real price chart."],["Common mistake","Trading off the rightmost ZigZag leg in real time without realizing it repaints and erases failed turns."]]},"t-99":{"title":"Volume Profile","line":"Volume Profile stacks traded volume horizontally by price level rather than vertically by time, helping you answer which price zones saw the heaviest trading activity over a selected window.","points":[["What you see","Price rotates between VAL and VAH and repeatedly slows near the POC line: Trading remains inside the historical 70% high-volume zone where two-sided activity was previously heaviest."],["What it does not mean","It does not make VAL an unbreakable floor or ensure bounces at the POC."],["Common mistake","Treating the POC or Value Area Low as a assured bounce level when POC is often a magnet where price chops sideways."]]},"t-100":{"title":"TPO/Market Profile","line":"TPO (Time Price Opportunity, or Market Profile) slices each session into fixed time blocks (such as 30-minute periods labeled A, B, C) and records which price rows were visited in each block, helping you answer where price spent the most time versus where it passed through briefly.","points":[["What you see","Letters stack wide around the middle price rows and taper at both ends in a bell shape: Price rotated repeatedly across the middle zone through most of the session's time blocks."],["What it does not mean","It does not ensure the next session will stay inside the same range or that volume matched time."],["Common mistake","Assuming more TPO letters automatically mean more traded volume—six quiet lunchtime hours at 101 print 12 letters on tiny volume, while one explosive 30-minute breakout at 105 prints just 1 letter on massive volume."]]},"t-101":{"title":"Volume Delta","line":"Volume Delta compares the volume classified as 'positive (up/buying-side)' against the volume classified as 'negative (down/selling-side)' inside a single candlestick: Volume Delta = Up Volume − Down Volume.","points":[["What you see","Price closes higher on positive Volume Delta: On this bar, price progress and positive-classified volume moved in the same direction."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee the next bar will also rise."],["Common mistake","Assuming positive Delta ensures rising prices when passive limit sell orders can absorb heavy market buying at resistance and send price lower."]]},"t-102":{"title":"CVD","line":"Cumulative Volume Delta (CVD) keeps a running ledger of single-bar Volume Delta values over a chosen anchor period to show whether positive-classified or negative-classified volume has been more persistent over time.","points":[["What you see","Within the same anchor period, both price and CVD reach new highs: Price advances and cumulative positive-classified volume are reinforcing each other over this period."],["What it does not mean","It is not a promise that price will keep rising afterward."],["Common mistake","Panicking when a Session-anchored CVD line drops vertically back to zero at the start of a new day, which is just the scheduled clock reset."]]},"t-103":{"title":"Volume Footprint","line":"A Volume Footprint chart opens up each candle to display classified sell volume and buy volume at every price row inside the bar, helping you answer where inside the candle trading was heaviest and which side was more aggressive.","points":[["What you see","Price steps higher with right-side buy volume exceeding left-side sell volume across multiple rows, closing near the high: Classified aggressive buying lifted offers across those price steps and held the advance into the close."],["What it does not mean","It does not ensure buying continues on the next bar or identify institutional accounts."],["Common mistake","Assuming a print of 500 units in one price cell came from a single whale order when it sums all trades at that price step across the bar (and every aggressive buy matched a passive limit sell)."]]},"t-104":{"title":"Advance/Decline","line":"The Advance/Decline Line (A/D Line) measures whether a broad universe of stocks is participating in a market move—it is not derived from a single stock's candlesticks.","points":[["What you see","Both the market index and the Advance/Decline Line reach new highs together: As the index climbs, a broad majority of constituent stocks are participating on a cumulative basis."],["What it does not mean","It does not guarantee every single stock rose today or that the index cannot pull back tomorrow."],["Common mistake","Thinking the Advance/Decline Line counts green versus red candles on a single stock rather than advancing versus declining stocks across an entire exchange."]]},"t-105":{"title":"New Highs/New Lows","line":"New Highs–New Lows counts how many stocks inside a defined universe reached a new high over a specified lookback period (commonly 52 weeks) versus how many reached a new low over that same period.","points":[["What you see","New High counts expand consecutively while New Lows remain near zero: A broadening group of constituent stocks is reaching the top of its lookback range."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the rally can never end or that every new entry today is low-risk."],["Common mistake","Comparing raw counts between a 3,000-stock exchange and a 500-stock index without converting to percentages."]]},"t-106":{"title":"Percent Above MA","line":"Percent of Stocks Above Moving Average measures what percentage of stocks inside a defined universe are currently trading above their own individual N-day moving average (for example, each stock's own 200-day simple moving average).","points":[["What you see","The percentage climbs steadily from 30% to 60%: A growing share of constituent stocks has reclaimed its own moving average, showing broadening participation."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean those stocks gained 30% in price, nor does it guarantee the improvement will continue."],["Common mistake","Mixing up thresholds for the 20-day, 50-day, and 200-day versions when the 20-day ratio swings between 15% and 85% much more often than the slow 200-day ratio."]]},"t-107":{"title":"Open Interest","line":"Open Interest (OI) counts the total number of derivative contracts (futures or perpetuals) that remain open and unsettled at a given moment, helping you answer whether total market positioning is expanding (new positions opening) or contracting (existing positions closing).","points":[["What you see","Price moves strongly while coin-denominated Open Interest rises steadily: More new contracts are being opened than closed as price moves, expanding total active positioning."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean the new contracts are 'all longs', because every new contract has both one long and one short."],["Common mistake","Claiming that rising Open Interest proves 'more buyers than sellers entered', forgetting that every single open contract matches one long with one short 1-to-1."]]},"t-108":{"title":"Funding Rate","line":"The Funding Rate shows the periodic fee percentage exchanged between long and short perpetual contract holders (commonly every 8 hours), helping you answer whether the perpetual trades rich or cheap relative to spot and which side pays to hold positions across the settlement timestamp.","points":[["What you see","Funding sits steadily near the exchange's baseline rate (such as +0.01% per 8 hours): Perpetual prices are tracking spot closely with normal baseline cost."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean price will stay flat or that holding a leveraged long is free."],["Common mistake","Calculating funding cost on initial margin instead of full leveraged notional value and underestimating how 10x leverage magnifies funding drain tenfold."]]},"t-109":{"title":"Long/Short Accounts Ratio","line":"The Long/Short Accounts Ratio divides the number of net-long user accounts by the number of net-short user accounts within a defined platform sample, helping you answer whether more individual accounts are positioned long or short.","points":[["What you see","The Long/Short Accounts Ratio climbs above 2.0 while price grinds lower: Twice as many sampled accounts hold net-long positions as net-short positions, often as smaller accounts buy dips."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean longs have more money than shorts or that majority headcount will push price up."],["Common mistake","Thinking a 2.5 Accounts Ratio means there are 2.5 times more long contracts than short contracts when every contract has one long and one short (1-to-1)."]]},"t-110":{"title":"Liquidations","line":"Liquidations data records the volume or dollar value of leveraged positions forcefully closed by an exchange's risk engine after margin fell below maintenance requirements, helping you answer how much forced position unwinding accompanied a recent price move.","points":[["What you see","Price plunges with a long lower wick while a tall Long Liquidation bar prints and Open Interest drops: Falling prices triggered maintenance margin calls on leveraged longs, forcing market sell orders that reduced open contracts."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove every leveraged long has been wiped out or that price cannot fall further."],["Common mistake","Getting the execution direction backward and thinking Long Liquidations mean buying support when liquidating a long actually forces a market sell order."]]},"t-111":{"title":"Support and Resistance","line":"Support and Resistance mark horizontal price zones where past declines repeatedly paused or bounced (support) and past advances repeatedly stalled or pulled back (resistance), helping you answer where buyers or sellers previously stepped in noticeably.","points":[["What you see","Price pulls back into a prior 99–101 support zone, slows down, and prints lower wicks: Price is re-entering an area where buyers previously absorbed selling pressure."],["What it does not mean","It does not ensure a bounce; repeated tests can weaken a zone until it breaks."],["Common mistake","Treating support as a razor-thin number like 100.00 instead of a zone and getting whipsawed by a normal wick to 99.50."]]},"t-112":{"title":"Trend Lines and Channels","line":"Trendlines and Channels connect a sequence of rising swing lows (or falling swing highs) with a straight diagonal line and a parallel opposite boundary, helping you answer what slope price has been following and whether that pace is holding, steepening, or breaking down.","points":[["What you see","Price pulls back to a rising lower channel line for a third touch and turns back up: The prior pace of rising lows is still holding along the established slope."],["What it does not mean","It does not ensure the fourth or fifth touch will also hold."],["Common mistake","Drawing an unsustainably steep 80-degree trendline and calling a major bear market the moment price pauses sideways and crosses it."]]},"t-113":{"title":"Candlestick Patterns","line":"Candlestick Patterns read the relationship among Open, High, Low, and Close across one to three bars, helping you answer which side controlled the close and where intraday pushes were rejected during that interval.","points":[["What you see","After a multi-bar drop into support, a candle prints a small body near its high with a long lower wick (hammer shape): Sellers pushed price sharply lower intraday, but buyers recovered most of the loss before the close."],["What it does not mean","One candle alone does not confirm a trend reversal without follow-through on subsequent bars."],["Common mistake","Jumping in on a live unclosed candle that looks like a hammer mid-session, only to watch it close at the dead low as a full bearish bar."]]},"t-114":{"title":"Classical Chart Patterns","line":"Classic Chart Patterns group multi-bar swing outlines into shapes such as double tops/bottoms, head-and-shoulders, triangles, and flags, helping you answer how buyers and sellers are consolidating across multiple swings and which boundary break confirms a resolution.","points":[["What you see","Price stalls twice near 120 and then closes decisively below the intervening 110 trough (neckline): The second rally failed to set a higher high and breaking 110 creates a lower low, confirming the double-top structure."],["What it does not mean","It does not ensure price will reach the full measured target, and a move back above 110 can still invalidate the break."],["Common mistake","Shorting the second touch of a 120 high before price ever breaks the 110 neckline, which fights an intact uptrend."]]},"t-115":{"title":"Price Action","line":"Price Action (naked-chart analysis) reads swing highs and lows, push-versus-pullback candle behavior, and reactions at key levels directly from the price bars, helping you answer whether the market is stepping up, stepping down, or overlapping sideways without relying on lagging indicators.","points":[["What you see","Highs step up (100 → 106 → 112) and lows step up (96 → 102 → 107) with strong push bars and shallow pullbacks: Upward swings consistently cover more distance than counter-trend pullbacks, maintaining an uptrend structure."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean buying at 112 is risk-free; a break below the 107 higher low warns the sequence has faltered."],["Common mistake","Removing indicators without replacing them with objective swing and structure rules, which turns 'price action' into impulsive emotional guessing."]]},"t-116":{"title":"Dow Theory","line":"Dow Theory separates market movement into three scales—the primary trend (tide), secondary reactions (waves), and minor daily fluctuations (ripples)—while checking whether related averages confirm each other, helping you answer whether a pullback is a normal pause inside a broad trend or a structural reversal.","points":[["What you see","After a multi-month advance, price pulls back about half of the last swing and then resumes making higher highs: The pullback acted as a secondary reaction within an intact primary uptrend."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean every 50% drop is harmless; if the next bounce fails below the old high and breaks the reaction low, the primary trend is questioned."],["Common mistake","Expecting Dow Theory to catch the exact top tick or bottom tick when its confirmation rule (waiting for a lower high and a broken reaction low) is designed to confirm after a turn is already underway."]]},"t-117":{"title":"Elliott Wave Theory","line":"Elliott Wave Theory classifies trend-following moves into 5-wave impulses (waves 1, 3, 5 with the trend and 2, 4 against it) and counter-trend moves into 3-wave corrections (A, B, C), helping you answer which exact price level would falsify your current wave hypothesis.","points":[["What you see","After a proposed Wave 1 from 100 to 110, Wave 2 holds at 103 (above 100) and price breaks above 110: The pullback respected the Wave 1 origin at 100 and cleared 110, keeping the bullish impulse hypothesis alive."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove the count is unique; the move could also be Wave C of a larger A-B-C correction."],["Common mistake","Moving wave labels after the fact to excuse every broken rule instead of honoring the invalidation price."]]},"t-118":{"title":"Wyckoff Method","line":"The Wyckoff Method compares price swings and bar ranges with volume across a trading range, helping you answer whether selling pressure is drying up near range lows (accumulation candidate) or buying power is stalling near range highs (distribution candidate).","points":[["What you see","Second and third visits to the range low print narrower candles and much lower volume than the initial sell-off: Fewer sellers are pressing offers at the bottom of the range compared with the initial drop."],["What it does not mean","Quieter selling does not ensure an immediate breakout; price can remain in the range or still break down later."],["Common mistake","Forcing every sideways range to match every letter of a textbook schematic when real ranges often skip a Spring or break down outright."]]},"t-119":{"title":"Harmonic Patterns","line":"Harmonic Patterns measure Fibonacci retracement and extension ratios across five swing points and four legs (X-A-B-C-D), helping you answer whether a four-leg swing sequence matches a specific geometric template (such as a Gartley or Bat) and where its Potential Reversal Zone (PRZ) sits.","points":[["What you see","AB retraces ~0.618 of XA, BC retraces 0.382–0.886 of AB, and price pauses near the 0.786 XA retracement at D: The four swings satisfy standard Gartley ratio rules and price is testing the projected PRZ band."],["What it does not mean","Matching ratios makes the pattern valid by definition, but does not ensure price must reverse at D."],["Common mistake","Trading any visual M or W shape as a 'harmonic pattern' without measuring the leg ratios."]]},"t-120":{"title":"Market Structure Analysis","line":"Market Structure Analysis tracks the sequence of swing highs (HH/LH) and swing lows (HL/LL) on a chosen timeframe, helping you answer whether the trend staircase is extending in the same direction (Break of Structure, BOS) or whether the latest step has been broken.","points":[["What you see","Price launches from a Higher Low (HL) and closes above the prior Swing High (HH): Buyers completed another upward step (bullish BOS), keeping the rising swing sequence intact."],["What it does not mean","It does not prevent an immediate pullback after the breakout candle closes."],["Common mistake","Mixing daily swing highs with 5-minute swing lows on the same chart and getting contradictory signals."]]},"t-121":{"title":"Gann Theory","line":"Gann Tools (such as the Gann Fan and Gann Box) link price change and elapsed time bars into fixed-ratio diagonal lines, helping you answer whether price is advancing faster or slower than a chosen baseline pace (the 1×1 line).","points":[["What you see","With scale locked, price stays above the rising 1×1 line and tracks near the steeper 2×1 line: Price is gaining more per bar than the chosen 1×1 baseline speed."],["What it does not mean","It does not make the 1×1 line a magical barrier, since its position depends on the chosen scale ratio."],["Common mistake","Trusting a line that merely looks 45 degrees on an unlocked screen where zooming in or out shifts the line across price bars."]]},"t-122":{"title":"Chan Theory (Chan Lun)","line":"Chan Theory (Chanlun) applies a step-by-step geometric hierarchy—candle inclusion merging, top/bottom fractals, strokes, segments, and overlapping centers—helping you answer how price swings organize around and depart from defined overlapping zones.","points":[["What you see","Three consecutive strokes overlap between 102 and 106, forming a shaded Center box [ZD, ZG]: Price has rotated across the 102–106 band for three swings, establishing a defined overlapping center."],["What it does not mean","Not every sideways rectangle on a chart meets the strict three-stroke overlap rule."],["Common mistake","Installing multiple auto-drawing Chanlun scripts without realizing they code different stroke and segment rules, producing conflicting centers on the same chart."]]},"t-123":{"title":"Smart Money Concepts (SMC)","line":"SMC (Smart Money Concepts) links prior swing highs/lows (liquidity pools), structural breaks (BOS/CHOCH), and the last opposing candle before a strong displacement move (Order Block, OB) into a structured chart workflow, helping you answer where a strong move originated and which exact price level invalidates a pullback idea. 'Smart money' is a narrative label for price action; candlesticks cannot reveal institutional account identities.","points":[["What you see","After a bullish BOS above a prior high, price pulls back into the 100–102 origin candle box and turns back up: Buyers stepped in near the origin of the prior breakout leg while keeping above the box low."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove a specific bank left unfilled limit orders sitting inside that rectangle."],["Common mistake","Believing textbook lore that 'institutions must defend or return to every Order Block to close their underwater positions', which leads to holding losing trades without a stop when price slices straight through a box."]]},"t-124":{"title":"Inner Circle Trader (ICT)","line":"The ICT methodology combines specific New York session windows (Killzones), prior session highs/lows, and three-candle Fair Value Gaps (FVG) into a rule-based routine, helping you answer whether price swept a key high or low during an active session window and displaced sharply in the opposite direction.","points":[["What you see","During the active session window, price sweeps below the prior low and then rallies sharply, leaving a clear bullish FVG: Selling below the prior low was absorbed and fast upward displacement left no wick overlap between Candle 1 and Candle 3."],["What it does not mean","It does not ensure price will pull back to fill the FVG before continuing, nor does it ensure the FVG will hold if tested."],["Common mistake","Assuming the word 'Gap' in Fair Value Gap means zero trades occurred there or that every FVG acts as a magnet that must be filled."]]},"t-125":{"title":"Order Flow Analysis","line":"Order Flow Analysis separates executed trades (Time & Sales / Tape) from resting limit orders waiting in the order book (DOM / Depth of Market), helping you answer whether orders at a key price level are actually being consumed by trades or simply pulled (canceled) before execution.","points":[["What you see","Heavy market buy orders print repeatedly on the tape at 100.0, yet the 100.0 offer refills and price fails to advance (absorption): Passive limit sellers at 100.0 are absorbing the incoming market buy orders without letting price clear the level."],["What it does not mean","It does not mean 100.0 can never break; if the passive seller finishes selling, price can jump quickly."],["Common mistake","Trusting a massive limit buy wall on the Order Book as a safe floor when resting limit orders can be canceled in milliseconds at zero cost."]]},"t-126":{"title":"Auction Market Theory","line":"Auction Market Theory (AMT) views market movement as a continuous two-way auction searching for price zones where buyers and sellers willingly trade in size, helping you answer whether current prices are being accepted (balanced rotation) or rejected (imbalanced search for a new value area).","points":[["What you see","Price opens inside yesterday's VAH–VAL band and rotates back and forth across the middle POC: Participants continue to accept yesterday's price range, keeping the market in rotational balance."],["What it does not mean","Balance does not last forever; eventually one side breaks out and establishes acceptance outside the range."],["Common mistake","Confusing the statistical term 'Value Area' (where 70% of yesterday's trades occurred) with fundamental valuation and assuming prices below VAL are 'fundamentally cheap'."]]},"t-127":{"title":"Supply and Demand Zones","line":"Supply and Demand Zone analysis marks compact consolidation bases from which price previously departed with a rapid, wide-range move, helping you answer where a sharp historical imbalance originated and how price behaves if it revisits that base.","points":[["What you see","After two tight base candles, large bullish candles drive price away by several times the base's height: Buying strongly outweighed selling at that base, creating a well-defined candidate demand zone."],["What it does not mean","It does not prove unfilled institutional limit buy orders are still sitting in that box today."],["Common mistake","Drawing rectangles over every minor turn without requiring an explosive departure, until the entire chart is covered in boxes."]]},"t-128":{"title":"Trend Bars & Doji","line":"A trend bar has a body covering most of its range and a close near one end of the range; a doji has an open very close to its close and a small body, here taken as a body smaller than one tenth of the range.","points":[["Start here","First compare the body with the whole range, then note whether the close sits near the top, bottom or middle."],["Think of it as","Watch whether a person takes a large step forward or probes both ways and ends up back where they started."],["Common mistake","A common mistake is to read a doji directly as a reversal signal. It only shows a temporary balance in that bar."]]},"t-129":{"title":"Inside & Outside Bars","line":"An inside bar has a high below the previous high and a low above the previous low; an outside bar has a high above the previous high and a low below the previous low.","points":[["Start here","Open the data window and compare neighbouring highs and lows one by one, rather than estimating by eye."],["Think of it as","Check whether a small stick fits entirely inside the previous box or sticks out at both ends."],["Common mistake","A common confusion is to treat an inside bar as a harami. A harami compares bodies, while an inside bar compares whole highs and lows, shadows included."]]},"t-130":{"title":"Pin Bars","line":"A pin bar is a single candle whose main shadow is more than twice its body, with the body near one end of the range and a short or absent opposite shadow; a bullish pin bar has a long lower shadow and a body near the top, while a bearish pin bar has a long upper shadow and a body near the bottom.","points":[["Start here","Measure three parts first: main shadow, body and opposite shadow, rather than only noting a long shadow."],["Think of it as","Like reaching far for something and pulling your hand back; reaching far does not mean you held it."],["Common mistake","A common mistake is to call every long shadow a pin bar. Shadow length, small body, closing location and location must hold together, and a long shadow in the middle of a range carries less weight."]]},"t-131":{"title":"Breakouts & Failed Breakouts","line":"A breakout is a bar that closes beyond the prior range high (or low).","points":[["Start here","Draw the range boundaries from the highs and lows of the bars inside it."],["Think of it as","Like stepping out of a door and stepping straight back in because no one outside was waiting."],["Common mistake","The classic mistake is treating an intrabar poke through the boundary as a breakout and chasing it."]]},"t-132":{"title":"Pullbacks & Second Entries","line":"In a pullback within an uptrend, the first bar whose high exceeds the prior bar’s high is High 1 (H1).","points":[["Start here","Confirm the larger direction is up, with rising highs and lows."],["Think of it as","Like pausing twice while walking downstairs: the second pause, looking up, is a better hint that you are turning around."],["Common mistake","A common miscount is calling any bullish bar in the pullback an H1. H1 only asks whether the high exceeds the prior bar’s high, regardless of colour."]]},"t-133":{"title":"Wedges & Three Pushes","line":"Three pushes: three consecutive swing highs (or lows) in one direction, each beyond the last, with each advance smaller than the one before.","points":[["Start here","Find three swing highs (or lows) in the same direction within a trend."],["Think of it as","Like jumping up steps: the first jump is high, the second lower, the third barely makes it."],["Common mistake","Many traders assume a reversal as soon as they count three highs. Three pushes only show weakening momentum."]]},"t-134":{"title":"Trading Ranges","line":"A trading range is a stretch of bars in which price swings back and forth between roughly fixed upper and lower bounds without a persistent series of higher highs and higher lows (or lower highs and lower lows).","points":[["Start here","Find the upper and lower bounds where price turns, each touched at least twice."],["Think of it as","Like pacing a corridor: the walls at each end stop you until one day a door opens."],["Common mistake","Beginners often chase moves in the middle of a range, where direction is least clear, while breakouts at the edges often fail."]]},"t-135":{"title":"Measured Moves","line":"A measured move copies the price distance of the first leg (A to B) from the end of the pullback C in the same direction, giving a reference level D = C + (B − A) for a rise, or D = C − (A − B) for a fall.","points":[["Start here","Mark the first leg’s start A and end B, and measure B − A."],["Think of it as","Like using the length of your first stride to guess where the second will land."],["Common mistake","The most common mistake is treating D as a guaranteed target. It is only a reference."]]},"t-136":{"title":"Channel Lines & Overshoots","line":"In a rising channel, the channel line is drawn parallel to the trend line on the side of the highs.","points":[["Start here","Draw the trend line through two or more lows and a parallel channel line through a high."],["Think of it as","Like a car running wide on a bend: either it really accelerates away, or it bounces off the guard rail."],["Common mistake","Chasing every break above the channel line as acceleration is a common error."]]},"t-137":{"title":"Climaxes & Exhaustion","line":"A climax is a run of unusually large bars in the trend’s direction at the end of a move, with ranges well above the recent average (here, more than twice the average range of the prior 20 bars), usually on rising volume.","points":[["Start here","Compute the average range of roughly the last 20 bars as the “normal size”."],["Think of it as","Like the last sprint of a marathon: afterwards the runner usually stops to catch breath."],["Common mistake","Calling a top on one big bullish bar is a common misjudgement."]]},"t-138":{"title":"Gaps","line":"An up gap is when a bar’s low is above the prior bar’s high, leaving a price zone with no trading between them; a down gap is the reverse.","points":[["Start here","Compare a bar’s low with the prior bar’s high (reverse for a down gap)."],["Think of it as","Like a missing stair: your foot lands directly on the next level up."],["Common mistake","“Gaps always get filled” is a common saying but not a rule: many breakaway gaps stay open for a long time, and crypto bars rarely gap, so stock gap theory does not carry over."]]},"t-139":{"title":"Multi-timeframe Analysis","line":"Multi-timeframe analysis uses a higher timeframe to set the context (trend direction and key levels) and a lower timeframe to watch how price behaves at those levels.","points":[["Start here","Pick a pair of timeframes about 4–6× apart, such as 4-hour and 1-hour."],["Think of it as","Like checking the map to choose the city, then the street plan to choose the turning."],["Common mistake","The most common error is flipping between timeframes until one agrees with what you already believe."]]},"t-140":{"title":"Engulfing Patterns","line":"Bullish engulfing: the prior bar is bearish, the current bar is bullish, and the current body fully covers the prior body (it opens at or below the prior close and closes above the prior open).","points":[["Start here","Check location first: a bullish engulfing matters only after a decline."],["Think of it as","Like a tug-of-war where the other team gains a step and you pull it back plus a bit more in one go."],["Common mistake","The most common misuse is treating an engulfing pattern in the middle of a sideways range as a reversal signal."]]},"t-141":{"title":"Hammer & Hanging Man","line":"Shape: a lower shadow at least twice the body, a short upper shadow (no longer than the body), and the body in the upper part of the range.","points":[["Start here","Measure the body and both shadows and check the ratios."],["Think of it as","The same umbrella is a sunshade on a sunny day and a rain umbrella in the rain; it depends on the weather."],["Common mistake","The most common confusion is recognising only the shape and ignoring location, calling a bar in the middle of a rally a bullish hammer."]]},"t-142":{"title":"Shooting Star & Inverted Hammer","line":"Shape: an upper shadow at least twice the body, a short lower shadow (no longer than the body), and the body in the lower part of the range.","points":[["Start here","Measure the body and both shadows and check the ratios."],["Think of it as","Like throwing a ball up into the wind: on a hilltop it shows strong wind; in a valley it shows someone trying to throw higher."],["Common mistake","Treating every long upper shadow in a rally as a top is a common misreading."]]},"t-143":{"title":"The Doji Family","line":"A doji has a body smaller than 10% of its range.","points":[["Start here","Compute the body as a share of the range and confirm it is under 10%."],["Think of it as","Like a long tug-of-war in which the rope ends up back at the start; who wins is decided in the next round."],["Common mistake","Treating a gravestone as a sell signal or a dragonfly as a buy signal is the most common misuse."]]},"t-144":{"title":"Morning & Evening Stars","line":"Morning star: the first bar is a long bearish bar; the second is a “star” whose body is less than a third of the first body; the third is bullish and closes above the midpoint of the first body.","points":[["Start here","Find a long bar in the trend’s direction and note its body midpoint."],["Think of it as","Like the darkest moment of the night, a pause, and then the sky starts to brighten."],["Common mistake","A common loosening is calling any “big down, small, big up” sequence a morning star without checking that the third bar closes past the first body’s midpoint."]]},"t-145":{"title":"Three White Soldiers & Three Black Crows","line":"Three white soldiers: three consecutive bullish bars, each closing above the prior close, each with an upper shadow no more than 30% of its range (closing near the high).","points":[["Start here","Find three consecutive bars of the same colour and check that closes rise (or fall) each time."],["Think of it as","Like climbing three steps with firm footing; after hurrying, you still need a breather."],["Common mistake","Chasing three white soldiers after a long rally is a common error."]]},"t-146":{"title":"Harami","line":"Harami: the second bar’s body lies entirely within the first bar’s body (touching the edge counts), the second body is less than half the first, and the two usually have opposite colours.","points":[["Start here","Find a bar with a large body and note its top and bottom."],["Think of it as","Like a big bag holding a small box: you compare the boxes, not the handles."],["Common mistake","The most common error is confusing a harami with an inside bar: a harami compares bodies, an inside bar compares the whole high and low."]]},"t-147":{"title":"Dark Cloud Cover & Piercing Pattern","line":"Dark cloud cover: the first bar is a long bullish bar; the second trades above the first bar’s high, then falls back to close as a bearish bar below the midpoint of the first body but above the first open.","points":[["Start here","Find a long bullish bar and compute its body midpoint."],["Think of it as","Like reaching a new height and then slipping back below the halfway point of the climb."],["Common mistake","Calling a bearish bar that closes above the midpoint a dark cloud cover is a common loosening."]]},"t-148":{"title":"Head & Shoulders","line":"Head and shoulders top: of three swing highs, the middle “head” is higher than the left and right “shoulders”; the two lows between them define the neckline.","points":[["Start here","Find three highs and confirm the middle one is highest."],["Think of it as","Like a hike where your third climb is clearly lower than the second: your energy is fading."],["Common mistake","The two most common mistakes are calling the pattern before the right shoulder has finished, and treating the reference distance as a guaranteed target."]]},"t-149":{"title":"Double Tops & Bottoms","line":"Double top: two similar swing highs (the second may be slightly higher or lower; here within 1%) with a swing low between them.","points":[["Start here","Find two similar highs and check the gap (here, within 1%)."],["Think of it as","Like pushing a door twice without it opening; until it is locked, the third push might work."],["Common mistake","Selling as soon as the second high appears is the most common premature call."]]},"t-150":{"title":"Triangles","line":"A triangle forms when lines through the swing highs and through the swing lows converge to the right.","points":[["Start here","Find at least two swing highs and two swing lows and draw a line through each set."],["Think of it as","Like two walls slowly closing in: you will leave by one side eventually, but which side first is uncertain."],["Common mistake","Assuming an ascending triangle must break upward is a common oversimplification."]]},"t-151":{"title":"Flags & Pennants","line":"A flag starts with a sharp “pole” (a few large bars), followed by a small parallel channel sloping against the pole (the flag) that retraces no more than half the pole.","points":[["Start here","Find the pole: several large bars in one direction."],["Think of it as","Like a sprinter jogging a couple of steps to catch breath before running on."],["Common mistake","Calling any pullback a flag without checking that the pole was sharp and the retracement shallow is a common error."]]},"t-152":{"title":"Rectangles","line":"A rectangle forms when price moves between two roughly horizontal lines, touching the top at least twice and the bottom at least twice; a close beyond either is a breakout.","points":[["Start here","Draw the two horizontal lines, each touched at least twice."],["Think of it as","Like going up and down the stairs between two floors until one day you leave the building."],["Common mistake","Assuming a rectangle must break in the direction of the prior trend is a common misconception."]]},"t-153":{"title":"Rising & Falling Wedges","line":"Rising wedge: swing highs and lows both rise, but the lows rise faster, so the two lines converge upward; a close below the lower line is a downside break.","points":[["Start here","Connect at least two highs and two lows and confirm both lines slope the same way and converge."],["Think of it as","Like cycling uphill on a road that keeps getting narrower and harder."],["Common mistake","Calling every upward-converging move a rising wedge without comparing slopes is a common error."]]},"t-154":{"title":"Cup and Handle","line":"Cup and handle: price falls from a high (the rim), forms a rounded bottom (the cup, not a V), and returns near the rim; it then pulls back slightly to form a “handle” whose retracement is less than half the cup’s depth.","points":[["Start here","Find the rim and the cup bottom and confirm the bottom is rounded, not a V."],["Think of it as","Like the side view of a teacup: a rounded bottom, a rim and a small handle."],["Common mistake","Drawing a sharp V-shaped drop and recovery as a cup, or ignoring an overly deep handle, strips the pattern of its meaning."]]},"t-155":{"title":"Rounding Bottoms & V Reversals","line":"A rounding bottom forms when a decline slows, price lingers sideways at the lows for a while, and then gradually rises, tracing a gentle U.","points":[["Start here","Look at the speed of the decline: gradually slowing or sharp."],["Think of it as","A rounding bottom is a ship slowly turning around; a V reversal is a ball bouncing straight off the floor."],["Common mistake","Trying to “call the V” and buy during a sharp fall is the most common mistake."]]},"t-156":{"title":"Divergence","line":"Regular bearish divergence: price’s second swing high is above the first, while the indicator’s value at that same bar is below its first high; regular bullish divergence is the reverse.","points":[["Start here","Find two swing highs (or lows) on price."],["Think of it as","Like a speedometer showing the car slowing while it keeps rolling forward; you still have to see whether it stops."],["Common mistake","The two most common mistakes are matching a price high with a different indicator peak, and treating divergence as an immediate sell signal."]]},"t-157":{"title":"Volume and Price","line":"Volume–price analysis sorts each move into four combinations: rising price on rising volume, rising price on falling volume, falling price on rising volume, and falling price on falling volume.","points":[["Start here","Split the move into segments: a rise, a pullback or a decline."],["Think of it as","Like watching a parade: does the crowd grow as it moves forward, or thin out?"],["Common mistake","Treating “rising volume on a rally is always good, falling volume always bad” as a rule is a common misconception."]]},"t-158":{"title":"TD Sequential","line":"TD Sequential has two phases.","points":[["Start here","Start from a price flip: the prior bar fails the condition and the current bar meets it."],["Think of it as","Like counting laps: after many laps a runner may tire, but that does not mean they stop now."],["Common mistake","“It reverses at 9” is the most common misuse."]]},"t-159":{"title":"Heikin Ashi","line":"Heikin Ashi recalculates each bar: HA close = (open + high + low + close) ÷ 4; HA open = (prior HA open + prior HA close) ÷ 2; HA high = the maximum of the real high, HA open and HA close; HA low is the minimum.","points":[["Start here","Switch the chart type to Heikin Ashi."],["Think of it as","Like drawing a bumpy road as a smooth line of average speed: nicer to look at, but not the real speed at every moment."],["Common mistake","Using Heikin Ashi closes as real prices for stops or backtests is the most common and most dangerous mistake."]]},"t-160":{"title":"Renko Charts","line":"A Renko chart draws a brick only when price has moved a fixed amount (the brick size): a close at least one brick above the top of the last brick adds an up brick; a reversal needs a close at least two bricks below the top (one brick below the bottom) to add a down brick.","points":[["Start here","Choose the brick size (fixed or ATR-based) and record it."],["Think of it as","Like making a note only when you climb a whole step; walking back and forth on the same step does not count."],["Common mistake","The most common misunderstandings are ignoring the choice of brick size and thinking the spacing of bricks reflects time."]]},"t-161":{"title":"Point & Figure Charts","line":"A point and figure chart records rises as columns of X and declines as columns of O, each box worth a fixed price amount (the box size).","points":[["Start here","Choose the box size and reversal amount (commonly three boxes) and record them."],["Think of it as","Like bookkeeping on graph paper: you only write an entry when the amount changes by a full box, and time is not recorded."],["Common mistake","Changing the box size or reversal amount but keeping the same conclusions is a common error."]]},"t-162":{"title":"Cost Distribution (Chip Distribution)","line":"Cost distribution (“chip distribution”) is an estimate: it assumes each day’s trading replaces part of the existing holdings according to turnover, allocates volume across the day’s price range, accumulates it day by day while decaying older “chips”, and arrives at where current holders’ costs are roughly distributed.","points":[["Start here","Confirm whether you are looking at cost distribution or a volume profile, and the platform’s assumptions."],["Think of it as","Like estimating, from the number of people entering and leaving a cinema each hour, what time the people still inside arrived."],["Common mistake","The most common misunderstanding is treating cost distribution as real position data, as in “the big players’ cost is right here”."]]},"t-163":{"title":"Basis & Premium","line":"This lesson uses the common crypto convention: basis = futures price − spot price (some texts use spot − futures; check the platform), and premium rate = basis ÷ spot price.","points":[["Start here","Make sure you compare the same underlying at the same moment."],["Think of it as","Like the price difference between booking next year’s flight and buying today’s: the gap reflects demand for the future and the cost of money."],["Common mistake","Reading a positive premium as a “bullish signal” is a common misconception."]]},"t-164":{"title":"Order Book Depth & Liquidity Heatmaps","line":"Order book depth lists the resting buy and sell orders at each price; a liquidity heatmap plots those resting orders over time as colours, brighter where more size rests.","points":[["Start here","Confirm which exchange and pair the book belongs to; one exchange is only part of the market."],["Think of it as","Like a “we buy in bulk” sign in a shop window: it can be taken down at any time, and the sign is not a trade."],["Common mistake","Treating bright heatmap bands as guaranteed support or resistance is the most common misconception."]]},"t-165":{"title":"Darvas Box","line":"Darvas box: after price makes a new high, if the next three bars fail to exceed it, that high becomes the box top; once the top is set, if a subsequent low is not broken by the next three bars, that low becomes the box bottom.","points":[["Start here","In a rally, find a new high and check that the next three bars fail to exceed it: that is the top."],["Think of it as","Like the landings of a staircase: rest on one, then climb to the next."],["Common mistake","Drawing boxes freehand without the rules, or “anticipating” a breakout before the top is confirmed, strips the method of meaning."]]},"t-166":{"title":"Turtle Trading","line":"Turtle trading is a rule-based trend-following system.","points":[["Start here","Use Donchian channels to mark the 20-bar (or 55-bar) high and the 10-bar (or 20-bar) low."],["Think of it as","Like a strict recipe: heat, quantities and timing are fixed, not adjusted by feel."],["Common mistake","Copying only the breakout entry while ignoring ATR-based sizing and the exit rules is the most common distortion."]]},"t-167":{"title":"Triple Screen","line":"Triple Screen assigns jobs to three timeframes about five times apart: screen one (long, e.g. weekly) uses a trend indicator to set the only direction to trade; screen two (intermediate, e.g. daily) uses an oscillator to find pullbacks against that trend, such as an oversold reading in an uptrend; screen three (short or intraday) uses price itself for entry, such as a buy stop above the prior bar’s high.","points":[["Start here","On the long timeframe, set direction with a trend indicator (such as the slope of the MACD histogram)."],["Think of it as","Like three security gates: first you are allowed into the building, then onto the floor, and only then can you open the door."],["Common mistake","Using the screens out of order, or acting on intermediate signals while the long timeframe points the other way, removes the filtering that is the system’s point."]]},"t-168":{"title":"Moving Average Alignment","line":"Bullish alignment: the short moving average is above the medium, and the medium above the long (such as MA5 > MA10 > MA20), with all three rising; bearish alignment is the reverse.","points":[["Start here","Add three moving averages of different lengths, such as 5, 10 and 20."],["Think of it as","Like three people climbing a hill in a line, the fastest in front, all heading up."],["Common mistake","Assuming a bullish alignment means prices will keep rising, and chasing long after it formed, is a common misconception."]]},"t-169":{"title":"Trend vs Range","line":"A trend is a stretch of bars in which swing highs and lows advance in one direction (higher highs and higher lows in an uptrend), most closes sit on one side, and moving averages slope clearly.","points":[["Start here","Mark recent swing highs and lows and see whether they advance in one direction."],["Think of it as","Like telling a steady tailwind from gusts blowing every which way: you set the sails differently."],["Common mistake","Chasing moving-average crossovers in a range, or fading overbought and oversold readings in a strong trend, are the most common cases of using the right tool in the wrong place."]]},"t-170":{"title":"Cutting Losses","line":"Cutting a loss means writing down, before entry, the condition that proves the trade wrong (the invalidation, either a price or a closing condition) and exiting as planned when it happens, rather than delaying because you do not want to admit the loss.","points":[["Start here","Before entry, write the invalidation: which price or close shows the idea is wrong."],["Think of it as","Like knowing where the brake is before you drive: not because you plan to crash, but so you can stop if something goes wrong."],["Common mistake","Moving the stop further away, or refusing to sell because “I have already lost so much,” is how small losses become large ones."]]},"t-171":{"title":"Averaging Down vs Adding to Winners","line":"Averaging down means buying more at a lower price while a position is losing, which lowers the average cost.","points":[["Start here","Write down the trade’s invalidation price and recompute total risk before any add."],["Think of it as","Averaging down is loading more cargo onto a leaking boat in the hope it reaches shore sooner; adding to winners is raising another sail in a following wind."],["Common mistake","Averaging all the way down lowers the average cost but multiplies total risk."]]},"t-172":{"title":"Expectancy & Win Rate","line":"Expectancy (average result per trade) = win rate × average win − (1 − win rate) × average loss, with the average loss taken as a positive number.","points":[["Start here","Record at least several dozen trades and compute the win rate, average win and average loss, including fees and slippage."],["Think of it as","Like running a shop: the number of sales each day matters less than income minus costs at the end of the month."],["Common mistake","Chasing a high win rate while ignoring loss size, or drawing conclusions from a dozen trades, a sample far too small to rule out luck."]]},"t-173":{"title":"Trading Psychology & Biases","line":"Common biases in trading include the gambler’s fallacy (thinking a run of gains means a fall is “due,” or a run of losses means a win is due), loss aversion (a loss hurts more than an equal gain pleases), the disposition effect (selling winners too early and holding losers too long), anchoring (judging cheap or expensive against a past price) and hindsight bias (treating explanations written afterwards as if they were foreseeable).","points":[["Start here","Before trading, write the reason, the invalidation and the target, so you cannot invent reasons afterwards."],["Think of it as","Like not keeping snacks at home when dieting: not because you believe you have no appetite, but because you admit you do and arrange things in advance."],["Common mistake","Believing that knowing about biases is enough to avoid them."]]},"t-174":{"title":"Liquidity & Market Impact","line":"Liquidity is the ability to buy or sell a given quantity without moving the price much, commonly gauged by the bid–ask spread, order-book depth and volume.","points":[["Start here","Before ordering, check the spread and the size on the first few levels, and estimate how many levels your quantity will consume."],["Think of it as","Like buying every tomato at a small market stall at once: the first few kilos are at the normal price, then the seller starts raising it."],["Common mistake","Assuming you can always trade at the chart price just from candles and volume, or sending one large market order in a thin market and getting filled at ever worse prices."]]},"t-175":{"title":"What a Stock Is","line":"A share of stock is a unit of ownership in a company.","points":[["Start here","Know which company you are buying, where it is listed and its ticker."],["Think of it as","Like a few friends opening a noodle shop and splitting profits by how much each put in; a listed company simply has thousands of such partners, and their stakes can change hands in the market at any time."],["Common mistake","Judging cheap or expensive by the share price, for example thinking a 2-dollar stock is “cheaper” than a 200-dollar one."]]},"t-176":{"title":"Exchanges, Brokers & Trading Rules","line":"An exchange matches buyers and sellers (for example the NYSE, Nasdaq, and the Shanghai and Shenzhen exchanges); ordinary investors open an account with a broker to place orders.","points":[["Start here","Choose the market: A-shares, Hong Kong, US stocks or crypto, each with different hours."],["Think of it as","Like driving in different countries: it is still driving, but which side of the road and the speed limit depend on local rules."],["Common mistake","Assuming every market lets you buy and sell the same day at any time, or ignoring minimum commissions and trading tiny amounts so often that fees eat most of the gains."]]},"t-177":{"title":"Placing Orders: Market vs Limit","line":"A market order fills immediately at the best price available, guaranteeing execution but not price.","points":[["Start here","Decide first whether filling matters more than the price or the reverse."],["Think of it as","A market order is telling the stallholder “a kilo at whatever you charge now”; a limit order is saying “I will buy if it drops to 8 a kilo, otherwise not yet.”"],["Common mistake","Assuming a limit order will fill at that price, or that a buy limit fills at the price you typed."]]},"t-178":{"title":"Indexes & ETFs","line":"A stock index turns the prices of a basket of stocks into one number representing a market or sector, such as the S&P 500, the Nasdaq-100 or the CSI 300.","points":[["Start here","Check which stocks the index holds, how many, and how they are weighted."],["Think of it as","An index is like a class’s average score; an ETF is like buying the whole class’s homework bundled as one."],["Common mistake","Expecting your own stock to rise because the index did, or comparing index points with an ETF’s price directly."]]},"t-179":{"title":"What Crypto Assets Are","line":"Crypto assets are digital assets recorded on a blockchain: an ordered chain of transaction records maintained by many nodes, which makes rewriting history costly.","points":[["Start here","Confirm the asset’s name, ticker and which blockchain it runs on (assets with the same name may exist on several chains)."],["Think of it as","A blockchain is like a public ledger of which everyone keeps a copy; to rewrite an old page you would have to change most people’s copies at once."],["Common mistake","Calling a token cheap by its unit price, for example thinking a 0.001-dollar token has more room to rise than a 1-dollar one."]]},"t-180":{"title":"Wallets & Exchange Custody","line":"Custodial: assets sit in an exchange account, the exchange holds the private keys, and you sign in with your credentials.","points":[["Start here","Decide which assets stay on the exchange (to trade) and which move to your own wallet (to hold)."],["Think of it as","Custody is like keeping cash in someone else’s safe-deposit box with the shop holding the key; self-custody is a safe at home, and if you lose the key no one can open it for you."],["Common mistake","Saving the recovery phrase as a screenshot in your photos or cloud drive, or trusting “support staff” who ask for your phrase or private key."]]},"t-181":{"title":"Stablecoins Basics","line":"A stablecoin is a crypto token designed to hold a target price, most often 1 US dollar.","points":[["Start here","Find out the type and issuer: reserve-backed, overcollateralized or algorithmic."],["Think of it as","Like a store gift card that says it is worth 100: whether it really is depends on the store still being around and having money behind it."],["Common mistake","Treating stablecoins as risk-free cash: the algorithmic UST depegged and collapsed in 2022, and in March 2023 USDC briefly fell below 1 dollar when a bank holding part of its reserves failed."]]},"t-182":{"title":"Spot, Margin & Leveraged Contracts","line":"Spot means buying the asset itself with your full funds; a drop is only a paper loss and you are not forced out (unless you borrowed).","points":[["Start here","Know whether you are trading spot or a contract, and read the leverage shown on the order screen."],["Think of it as","Spot is like buying a house with your own money: a price drop just shrinks it on paper."],["Common mistake","Seeing only magnified gains and not equally magnified losses, or believing the right direction cannot lose, then being liquidated before price comes back."]]},"t-183":{"title":"Beginner Safety: Scams & Money Management","line":"Common scams include investment schemes or “copy-trade” offers promising steady high returns, impostors posing as support or friends to obtain codes and recovery phrases, fake websites and apps (phishing), “pig-butchering” schemes where a stranger builds trust over time before steering you into an investment, and pump-and-dump chat groups.","points":[["Start here","Treat “capital guaranteed,” “sure profit,” “fixed daily returns” and “limited spots, pay now” as red flags."],["Think of it as","Like travelling: guard your passport and wallet before studying the sights; lose the wallet and the best itinerary falls apart."],["Common mistake","Sending money because someone posted profit screenshots, or borrowing and adding leverage to “win it back,” turning one loss into a problem that affects your life."]]},"t-184":{"title":"Reading Financial Statements","line":"The three financial statements are the standardized reporting framework: the Income Statement (Net Income = Revenue - Expenses - Taxes) measures operational profitability over a period; the Balance Sheet (Assets = Liabilities + Equity) captures financial condition at a point in time; and the Cash Flow Statement (Ending Cash = Beginning Cash + Operating + Investing + Financing Cash Flows) tracks actual cash inflows and outflows.","points":[["Start here","Search the issuer's latest 10-K or 10-Q filing on official platforms like SEC EDGAR, CNINFO, or HKEXnews."],["Think of it as","The income statement is a school report card, the balance sheet is a net worth snapshot, and the cash flow statement is the circulatory bloodstream."],["Common mistake","Confusing accrual accounting net income with liquid cash in the bank, overlooking that uncollected receivables or obsolete inventories can trigger insolvency."]]},"t-185":{"title":"Valuation Multiples: P/E, P/B, and P/S","line":"Valuation multiples evaluate relative share pricing against operational fundamentals.","points":[["Start here","Open your analytical tool to locate P/E (TTM), Forward P/E, P/B, and P/S data for the target ticker."],["Think of it as","Multiples are like price per square foot in real estate, standardizing different property sizes into a comparable unit price."],["Common mistake","Assuming a low P/E ratio automatically represents a bargain, ignoring that permanent secular decay or debt distress often justifies steep market discounts."]]},"t-186":{"title":"Earnings Season & Guidance","line":"Earnings season is the concentrated period when public companies report periodic financials; Consensus Estimates represent the mean analyst forecast for revenue and EPS; Guidance is management's official forward-looking performance forecast.","points":[["Start here","Verify whether the earnings announcement is scheduled Before Market Open (BMO) or After Market Close (AMC) on official calendars."],["Think of it as","Earnings are like an exam where expectations are set at 95; scoring a 90 is viewed by the market as a disappointment."],["Common mistake","Assuming past earnings growth guarantees an immediate stock rally, ignoring that forward guidance revisions dictate valuation multiples."]]},"t-187":{"title":"Dividends & Corporate Actions","line":"Ex-dividend and ex-rights represent the regulatory adjustment of a stock's market trading price following cash dividend distributions, bonus shares, or rights offerings.","points":[["Start here","Identify the key corporate timeline: Announcement Date, Record Date, Ex-Dividend Date, and Payment Date."],["Think of it as","Dividends resemble transferring cash between pockets in the same wallet; total net worth remains exactly unchanged."],["Common mistake","Assuming cash dividends represent free windfalls, ignoring that stock prices adjust downward dollar-for-dollar upon the ex-dividend date."]]},"t-188":{"title":"IPOs & New Listings","line":"An Initial Public Offering (IPO) is the initial sale of equity by a private corporation to the institutional and retail public.","points":[["Start here","Review the official Prospectus (Form S-1 or equivalent) on EDGAR or exchange portals to inspect financial sustainability and risk factors."],["Think of it as","An IPO is like a new property launch; the offering price is set by the developer, but market resale prices depend strictly on buyer demand."],["Common mistake","Assuming newly listed IPO shares represent risk-free guaranteed profits, ignoring that market-based pricing routinely produces first-day opening losses."]]},"t-189":{"title":"Options Basics","line":"An option is a standardized derivative contract conveying the right, but not the obligation, to buy (Call) or sell (Put) an underlying asset at a designated strike price on or before a specified expiration date.","points":[["Start here","Open the standardized Option Chain to review strike prices, bid-ask spreads, and expiration cycles."],["Think of it as","Buying options is like buying car insurance: the buyer pays a premium that decays to zero if no disaster occurs, but pays out if a crash happens."],["Common mistake","Assuming that being directionally right guarantees a profit, overlooking that failing to outpace Theta decay or an IV crush can still wipe out 100% of the option premium."]]},"t-190":{"title":"Backtesting & Overfitting","line":"Backtesting is the quantitative simulation of a codified trading rule across historical market data; Net Backtest Return = Gross Simulated Profit - Cumulative Commissions - Cumulative Slippage.","points":[["Start here","Split historical data strictly into In-Sample (for development) and Out-of-Sample (for blind validation) segments."],["Think of it as","Overfitting is like memorizing last year's answer key letter-by-letter for this year's exam; any new question causes failure."],["Common mistake","Repeatedly tweaking indicator parameters across the entire dataset until the equity curve looks flawless, mistaking curve-fitted historical noise for predictive edge."]]},"t-191":{"title":"Trading Plan & Journal","line":"A Trading Plan is a pre-trade written rulebook specifying the target asset, entry trigger, position size (Max Acceptable Dollar Risk / Stop Distance per Share), invalidation stop-loss, and exit criteria; a Trading Journal is a post-trade ledger documenting execution metrics, rule deviations, and emotional states.","points":[["Start here","Draft a pre-trade plan before entry: define the ticker, objective trigger conditions, and maximum position size."],["Think of it as","A trading plan is a pilot's pre-flight checklist, while a trading journal is the post-flight black box recorder."],["Common mistake","Confusing a vague directional bias with a concrete trading plan, or selectively logging winning trades while ignoring losses, which destroys statistical feedback."]]},"t-192":{"title":"Seasonality & Calendar Effects","line":"Seasonality and Calendar Effects refer to empirical market anomalies where asset returns exhibit historical statistical deviations during specific months, quarters, days of the week, or holiday windows.","points":[["Start here","Identify whether a structural mechanism backs the pattern, such as year-end tax-loss harvesting, quarter-end window dressing, or physical commodity cycles."],["Think of it as","Seasonality is like historical climate averages: October is cooler on average, yet any single October day can still bring a heatwave."],["Common mistake","Treating a slight multi-decade statistical average as a guaranteed annual event, ignoring that a single macro regime shift can inflict heavy losses."]]},"t-193":{"title":"Margin Financing Balance","line":"Margin Financing and Securities Lending Balance represents the outstanding aggregate value of borrowed funds used to purchase securities (margin financing) plus borrowed securities sold short (securities lending).","points":[["Start here","Check official exchange portals (SSE, SZSE, or CSFC) prior to the morning open for T-1 aggregate and ticker-level margin balances."],["Think of it as","Margin balance is like the total tab of borrowed chips at a casino; a rising tab shows high excitement, not guaranteed future wins."],["Common mistake","Treating a rising margin balance as a predictive institutional buy signal, overlooking that retail margin debt typically lags price trends and peaks near market tops."]]},"t-194":{"title":"Dragon-Tiger List","line":"The Dragon-Tiger List (Statutory Trading Public Information) is an after-hours regulatory disclosure by Chinese stock exchanges listing the top five buying and selling brokerage branches or trading units for securities hitting abnormal thresholds in daily price deviation, amplitude, turnover, or 3-day cumulative deviation.","points":[["Start here","Access the 'Trading Public Information' portal on SSE, SZSE, or BSE websites after market close to inspect qualifying tickers."],["Think of it as","Copy-trading the Dragon-Tiger List is like placing bets based on yesterday's highlight reel; the winning move has already happened."],["Common mistake","Assuming a brokerage branch name represents a single mastermind trader, or believing 'Institutional Dedicated' seats guarantee long-term holding rather than next-day flipping."]]},"t-195":{"title":"Limit-up Boards","line":"A Limit-Up Board occurs when a security hits the maximum daily percentage price increase permitted by the stock exchange; Limit-Up Price = Round(Prior Close * (1 + Limit Percentage), Tick Size).","points":[["Start here","Verify the board's current statutory price limit: +/-10% for SSE/SZSE Main Boards (including ST/*ST risk-warning stocks, raised from +/-5% to +/-10% from July 6, 2026), +/-20% for STAR/ChiNext, and +/-30% for BSE."],["Think of it as","A limit-up bid wall is like a crowd of scalpers queued outside a store; it looks massive, but vanishes the second sentiment shifts."],["Common mistake","Treating a large resting Bid 1 order queue as guaranteed price support, forgetting that unfilled limit orders can be pulled in milliseconds right before a dump."]]},"t-196":{"title":"Blockchain & the Trilemma","line":"A blockchain is a ledger kept by many nodes and chained in time order: transactions from a period are packed into a “block”, and each block carries the hash (data fingerprint) of the previous one, so altering any page breaks every fingerprint after it.","points":[["Start here","Separate on-chain records from exchange balances; the latter are just the exchange’s internal bookkeeping."],["Think of it as","A village used to have one ledger kept by the headman; if he erred or was bribed, everyone suffered."],["Common mistake","Believing “on-chain means true”. A blockchain only makes records hard to alter after they are written."]]},"t-197":{"title":"Bitcoin vs Ethereum","line":"Bitcoin (BTC) is the first public chain, capped at 21 million coins, with new issuance halving about every 210,000 blocks (roughly four years); it is designed for simple, stable value transfer and storage.","points":[["Start here","Separate the uses: BTC is mainly for transfers and storing value; ETH also pays fees for on-chain applications."],["Think of it as","Bitcoin is like a vault that is rarely rebuilt, its rules seldom touched; Ethereum is like a city under constant expansion, with more opportunity and more construction risk."],["Common mistake","Assuming Ethereum’s lack of a cap means unlimited inflation, or that Bitcoin’s cap means it can only rise. Supply rules are one input."]]},"t-198":{"title":"Consensus & Chain Types","line":"A consensus mechanism is the set of rules plus rewards and penalties by which mutually distrustful nodes decide who writes the next block and what counts as valid.","points":[["Start here","Check which consensus the chain uses: PoW, PoS or a variant."],["Think of it as","PoW is like a quiz where whoever solves the puzzle first writes the page, and cheating means buying most of the machines; PoS is like a deposit scheme where the bookkeeper posts a bond and forfeits it for bad entries."],["Common mistake","Treating PoS as a savings product. Staking rewards pay for doing validation work and bearing slashing risk, and a price drop during the lock-up still means a loss."]]},"t-199":{"title":"Exchange Risk: Lessons from Mt. Gox, FTX & Bybit","line":"A centralized exchange (CEX) holds users’ assets and matches trades internally: the balance you see is a debt the exchange owes you, not coins in your own on-chain wallet.","points":[["Start here","Keep on the exchange only what you plan to trade soon; move long-term holdings to a wallet whose keys you control."],["Think of it as","Keeping coins on an exchange is like leaving cash at a private storage counter: convenient day to day, but if it fails or is robbed you are just one creditor in the queue."],["Common mistake","Believing an exchange is too big to fail. Mt. Gox in 2014 and FTX in 2022 were both leading exchanges."]]},"t-200":{"title":"Airdrops & Sybil Attacks","line":"An airdrop is a free distribution of tokens to qualifying addresses, typically based on past use of a product (a retroactive airdrop) or on completed tasks and accumulated points.","points":[["Start here","Confirm the announcement comes from the project’s official channels; beware fake sites asking you to connect a wallet to “claim”."],["Think of it as","An airdrop is like a mall giving loyal customers vouchers; a Sybil attack is one person queueing a hundred times in a hundred outfits, and once caught, all those vouchers are voided."],["Common mistake","Treating airdrops as risk-free income. Many “claim your airdrop” pages are phishing sites, and one wrong signature can drain a wallet."]]},"t-201":{"title":"Layer 2 & Rollups","line":"A layer 2 executes many transactions off the main chain and posts compressed results and proofs back to it (layer 1), cutting fees and raising speed while inheriting as much of the main chain’s security as possible.","points":[["Start here","Know which layer 2 you are using and whether it is optimistic or ZK."],["Think of it as","An optimistic rollup is like waving goods through and auditing later, reversing anything reported during the audit window; a ZK rollup is like every shipment carrying an inspection certificate that customs checks before release."],["Common mistake","Assuming a layer 2 is “as safe as mainnet”. Most are still decentralizing, and upgrade keys, sequencers and bridges can all be single points of failure."]]},"t-202":{"title":"Bridges & Oracles","line":"A cross-chain bridge “moves” assets between chains, usually by locking them on the source chain and minting an equal amount of wrapped tokens on the destination; if the bridge’s lock contract or signers are compromised, the wrapped tokens can lose their backing.","points":[["Start here","Before using a bridge, check how many signers control it, whether it is audited and whether it has had incidents."],["Think of it as","A bridge is like depositing gold in a vault in city A and receiving a claim ticket in city B; if the vault is robbed, the ticket is worthless."],["Common mistake","Treating a wrapped asset as identical to the native one. Wrapped ETH is a claim backed by a bridge."]]},"t-203":{"title":"Smart Contracts & Token Approvals","line":"A smart contract is a program on a blockchain that executes automatically when its conditions are met.","points":[["Start here","Before signing, read the wallet prompt: is it a transfer, an approval, or an off-chain permit signature?"],["Think of it as","An approval is like giving a cleaning company a spare key: handy for them to come in whenever, but if the company vanishes or the key is stolen, your home is exposed."],["Common mistake","Believing “if I don’t click transfer, I can’t lose money”. Approvals and off-chain signatures can hand over spending rights by themselves, and many phishing thefts happen on a signature users thought was just a login or verification."]]},"t-204":{"title":"Tokenomics: Supply, FDV & Unlocks","line":"Tokenomics covers how a token is issued, allocated, released and used.","points":[["Start here","Look at both circulating cap and FDV, and work out circulating supply as a share of the maximum."],["Think of it as","It is like a company listing only 10% of its shares while founders and investors hold the other 90% and can sell over the next few years; today’s price reflects supply and demand for that 10% alone."],["Common mistake","Judging by unit price. A USD 0.01 token with a trillion units still has a USD 10 billion FDV."]]},"t-205":{"title":"DEXs & AMMs","line":"A decentralized exchange (DEX) lets users trade directly from their own wallets through smart contracts, without handing assets to an exchange.","points":[["Start here","Check pool depth and the estimated price impact before trading; split large trades or use an aggregator."],["Think of it as","A pool is like a balance with apples on one side and oranges on the other: take apples and you must add oranges, and the more it tilts, the dearer the next apple."],["Common mistake","Treating the DEX price as the “market price”. In a shallow pool one trade can push the price far up or down."]]},"t-206":{"title":"DeFi Lending & Liquidation","line":"Decentralized lending protocols such as Aave and Compound let users deposit assets to earn interest or borrow other assets against deposits.","points":[["Start here","Before borrowing, work out the liquidation price: where the health factor equals 1."],["Think of it as","Like pawning gold for a loan: the pawnshop watches only the gold price, and once it falls far enough it automatically sells part of your gold to repay the debt, at a discount."],["Common mistake","Depositing just because the rate is high, while ignoring contract bugs, oracle failures and de-pegging. A high rate often signals higher risk or thin demand."]]},"t-207":{"title":"Staking, Liquid Staking & Restaking","line":"Staking locks tokens in a proof-of-stake network to help validate and earn rewards.","points":[["Start here","Know whether you hold the native token, a liquid staking token (LST) or a liquid restaking token (LRT)."],["Think of it as","Staking is like a fixed deposit; liquid staking is like getting a transferable certificate of deposit; restaking is pledging that certificate again as security for someone else to earn a second interest stream."],["Common mistake","Treating staking yield as risk-free interest. It pays for validation and slashing risk, and the more layers you stack, the higher the headline yield and the harder the true risk is to judge."]]},"t-208":{"title":"Greeks I: Delta & Gamma","line":"Delta (Δ) measures how much an option's theoretical price changes for a 1-unit move in the underlying asset; Call Delta ranges from 0 to 1, while Put Delta ranges from -1 to 0.","points":[["Start here","Locate the Delta and Gamma columns in the option chain and verify the sign convention (positive Delta for Calls, negative Delta for Puts)."],["Think of it as","If the underlying asset's price move is the distance traveled by a car, Delta is the car's current speed (how much the option price moves per dollar of stock movement), while Gamma is the acceleration pedal (how fast that speed itself changes)."],["Common mistake","Treating entry Delta as fixed over the life of the trade, or equating a 0.30 Delta with a literal 30% real-world chance of making money."]]},"t-209":{"title":"Greeks II: Theta & Vega","line":"Theta (Θ) measures the daily decline in an option's theoretical value due purely to the passage of one calendar day, holding other inputs constant (typically negative for option buyers).","points":[["Start here","Read Theta and Vega on the option chain, noting that Theta is quoted per calendar day while Vega reflects a 1-percentage-point move in IV."],["Think of it as","Think of an option's time value as an ice cube sitting outdoors: Theta is the steady drip of daily melting that speeds up as the deadline nears, while Vega is the outside weather—rising implied volatility refreezes and thickens the ice, whereas falling implied volatility is a sudden heatwave that shrinks it rapidly."],["Common mistake","Treating short-option Theta collection as effortless rent or assuming an option only changes by Theta when the underlying stays flat."]]},"t-210":{"title":"Implied Volatility, IV Rank & Volatility Crush","line":"Implied Volatility (IV) is the annualized expected volatility backed out from an option's live market price using a pricing model.","points":[["Start here","Compare current IV against its own 52-week history using IV Rank and IV Percentile rather than judging a standalone IV percentage in isolation."],["Think of it as","Think of IV as the live price of umbrellas before a storm; IV Rank marks where today's price sits on the ruler between the year's cheapest and most expensive price tags; IV Percentile counts how many days over the past year umbrellas cost less than today; and IV Crush is the morning the storm warning is canceled, when panic demand vanishes and prices drop instantly."],["Common mistake","Assuming an 80% IV Rank guarantees volatility will drop immediately, or buying pre-earnings options for direction alone while ignoring IV Crush."]]},"t-211":{"title":"Volatility Smile & Skew","line":"Volatility Smile and Volatility Skew (or Smirk) describe the pattern in which options on the same underlying and expiration date carry different Implied Volatilities (IV) across strike prices rather than a flat line: when both low-strike and high-strike options trade at higher IVs than at-the-money (ATM) options in a U-shaped curve, it is called a Volatility Smile; when IV slopes asymmetrically toward one side (commonly higher IV at lower strikes in equity index options), it is called Volatility Skew.","points":[["Start here","Hold expiration constant and plot Strike Price (or Delta) on the horizontal axis against Implied Volatility (IV) on the vertical axis to see whether the curve forms a symmetric U-smile or a tilted skew."],["Think of it as","Think of it as catastrophe insurance pricing: in equity markets, institutional investors hold massive long stock portfolios and fear sudden market crashes, so they persistently bid up out-of-the-money downside Puts as crash insurance while selling upside Calls—making low-strike IV much more expensive than high-strike IV and tilting the curve into a lopsided smirk (skew)."],["Common mistake","Seeing high IV on out-of-the-money downside Puts and assuming they are 'mispriced free money' to sell naked."]]},"t-212":{"title":"Covered Calls & Protective Puts","line":"A Covered Call combines holding the underlying asset (e.g., 100 shares of stock) with selling 1 corresponding Call option, collecting option premium in exchange for capping upside gains above the strike price—unlike an unhedged naked short call (see the \"Options Basics\" lesson on this site).","points":[["Start here","Before selling a Covered Call, verify that you hold the full underlying quantity (typically 100 shares per standard U.S. equity option contract) so the position does not become a naked short call."],["Think of it as","A Covered Call is like a homeowner collecting a non-refundable deposit in exchange for granting a buyer the right to purchase the house at a fixed cap price: you pocket the deposit today, but forfeit any appreciation above the cap."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a Covered Call for crash protection."]]},"t-213":{"title":"Vertical Spreads","line":"A Vertical Spread involves simultaneously buying 1 option and selling 1 option of the exact same type (both Calls or both Puts) on the same underlying asset and with the same expiration date, but at different strike prices.","points":[["Start here","Verify that both legs share the exact same underlying asset, expiration date, and option type (both Calls or both Puts), differing only by strike price."],["Think of it as","A debit vertical spread is like buying a concert ticket while immediately reselling the voucher for the late-night encore to someone else: the cash from selling the encore voucher lowers your upfront ticket cost and makes it easier to get your money's worth, in exchange for giving up any upside if the band plays an unforgettable two-hour encore."],["Common mistake","Oversizing positions simply because a vertical spread costs less per contract, or ignoring pin and assignment risk when the underlying finishes between the two strikes at expiration."]]},"t-214":{"title":"RWA: Real-World Assets On-Chain","line":"The process of mapping ownership or yield rights of off-chain traditional assets—such as government bonds, real estate, or receivables—onto the blockchain as tokens via legal structures and smart contracts.","points":[["Start here","Identify the underlying asset class: distinguish between liquid government bonds/money market funds and illiquid private credit or real estate."],["Think of it as","Think of RWA tokenization like a theater issuing numbered digital receipts for shares of a physical building's rental income: the building itself remains governed by real-world property laws and managers, while the digital receipts make transferring fractional shares fast—yet if the property manager misreports accounts or tenants default, the digital receipt alone cannot conjure up rent."],["Common mistake","Assuming tokenized assets are censorship-resistant—compliant RWA contracts typically include freeze and clawback permissions, and physical assets remain subject to legal enforcement."]]},"t-215":{"title":"DePIN: Token-Incentivised Infrastructure","line":"Decentralized Physical Infrastructure Networks (DePIN), a model that uses token incentives to coordinate individuals and nodes worldwide to contribute hardware resources—such as wireless hotspots, sensors, storage, or compute—to build and operate physical infrastructure.","points":[["Start here","Classify the resource type: determine whether the network provides physical resources (wireless, sensors, energy) or digital infrastructure (storage, compute, bandwidth)."],["Think of it as","Think of DePIN as a crowdsourced weather-station network: instead of a government agency spending millions to build every station itself, residents receive reward points for installing standardized balcony sensors—yet if no agricultural or logistics companies actually pay to buy the resulting weather data, the reward points lose value."],["Common mistake","Calculating static hardware payback periods using initial daily yields and peak token prices while ignoring reward dilution and token inflation."]]},"t-216":{"title":"On-Chain Identity: DID & SBT","line":"Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs) are cryptographic digital identity standards controlled directly by the user, while Soulbound Tokens (SBTs) are non-transferable on-chain tokens bound to an account to represent credentials, reputation, or affiliations.","points":[["Start here","Distinguish identifiers from credentials: recognize that a DID is the base identifier (e.g., did:ethr), whereas Verifiable Credentials (VCs) and SBTs are attestations attached to it."],["Think of it as","Think of a DID as a personal digital folder you hold the keys to, and SBTs as non-transferable diplomas or merit badges stamped inside: unlike collectible coins that anyone can buy, a diploma cannot be sold to someone else—proving your experience, while also meaning that if you leave the folder open in public, anyone can trace your life history."],["Common mistake","Equating a naming service (like .eth) with a complete decentralized identity—a domain is a human-readable alias, not a non-transferable credential."]]},"t-217":{"title":"New Token Standards: ERC-404, 6551, 3525","line":"Emerging token architectures building upon ERC-20 and ERC-721 to explore hybrid fungible/non-fungible liquidity (ERC-404), Token Bound Accounts (ERC-6551), and semi-fungible financial instruments (ERC-3525).","points":[["Start here","Identify the standard status: check on a block explorer whether the contract implements a Final EIP (ERC-3525), an EIP still under review (ERC-6551), or an experimental design outside the EIP process (ERC-404)."],["Think of it as","If ERC-20 is divisible cash and ERC-721 is an indivisible collector's stamp, ERC-404 is like a voucher that automatically issues a stamp whenever you hold a whole unit and shreds the stamp if you spend a fraction; ERC-6551 gives each stamp its own built-in lockbox; and ERC-3525 acts like reloadable gift cards from the same batch that can be split or merged by value."],["Common mistake","Assuming every \"ERC\" label implies formal Ethereum approval—ERC-404 has not passed the official EIP review process."]]},"t-218":{"title":"AI × Web3","line":"The intersection of Artificial Intelligence (AI) inference and automation with blockchain-based decentralized incentives, cryptographic verification, and on-chain settlement.","points":[["Start here","Identify the stack layer: distinguish between decentralized compute/data infrastructure, verifiable inference middleware (zkML/opML), and user-facing AI agents."],["Think of it as","Think of AI as an automated chef and blockchain as a transparent pantry ledger and cash register: the chef automates complex cooking tasks while the ledger tracks ingredient provenance and pays suppliers automatically—yet if an attacker slips the chef a deceptive note (prompt injection), the chef might empty the cash register into a stranger's pocket."],["Common mistake","Believing distributed consumer GPU networks can effortlessly replace hyperscale data centers for synchronous frontier model training."]]},"t-219":{"title":"DeSci: Decentralised Science","line":"Decentralized Science (DeSci), a movement using blockchains, DAOs, non-transferable credentials, and IP-NFTs to reform research funding, peer review, open data sharing, and intellectual property commercialization.","points":[["Start here","Evaluate scientific peer review: verify whether a research DAO uses qualified domain scientists to vet proposals rather than pure token-weighted voting."],["Think of it as","If traditional science is like waiting in a long line for a small grant committee and paying a tollbooth to publish your findings, DeSci is like an open research cooperative that logs lab notebooks in a public archive and shares future patent royalties via digital deeds—yet if voters cannot read the lab protocols, funds may flow to flashy pseudoscience."],["Common mistake","Assuming holding a DeSci governance token automatically grants direct legal patent ownership in every court."]]},"t-220":{"title":"A Map of the Web3 Industry","line":"A structural map of the Web3 industry organized by technical stack and business function, spanning base-layer infrastructure, middleware and tooling, user-facing applications, and supporting service ecosystems.","points":[["Start here","Map the four ecosystem layers: identify whether a project sits in base infrastructure, middleware/security, user applications, or research and compliance services."],["Think of it as","Think of the Web3 industry as a newly built digital port city: Layer 1 and Layer 2 engineers build the roads and power grid, oracle and audit teams run the waterworks and safety inspections, DeFi and consumer apps operate the shops and banks on main street, and researchers, community leads, and legal counsel serve as the city's newspapers, guides, and law offices."],["Common mistake","Believing only cryptography PhDs or Solidity coders can work in Web3—protocols rely equally on frontend/backend engineers, designers, researchers, and operations specialists."]]},"t-221":{"title":"Everyday Tools & Industry Jargon","line":"The five essential tool categories for daily Web3 research and operations (block explorers, data dashboards, self-custody wallets, multisigs, and testnet faucets) alongside core industry jargon.","points":[["Start here","Verify transactions on a block explorer: paste a TxHash or contract address to confirm execution status, gas paid, and whether source code is verified."],["Think of it as","Think of these five tools as a ship's navigation kit: a block explorer is sonar showing every underwater rock, a data dashboard is weather radar, a personal wallet is the captain's key, a multisig is a vault requiring three officers to turn their keys simultaneously, and a testnet faucet is free simulator fuel for practicing on dry land."],["Common mistake","Clicking sponsored search engine ads for wallets or explorers, which frequently lead to phishing clones that steal seed phrases."]]},"t-222":{"title":"Technical Roles in Web3","line":"The engineering career landscape in Web3 spanning smart contract development, security auditing, full-stack dApp integration, and core protocol/cryptography engineering.","points":[["Start here","Choose an entry track: leverage existing web engineering skills via Web3 frontend/indexing, or focus directly on smart contract development and auditing."],["Think of it as","Traditional web development is like renovating a restaurant where you can reprint a typo on the menu anytime; smart contract engineering and auditing are like building and inspecting a spacecraft before launch—once in orbit, structural flaws are extremely hard to patch."],["Common mistake","Copy-pasting contract templates without writing thorough unit and fuzz tests."]]},"t-223":{"title":"Operations & Business Roles in Web3","line":"The non-technical career landscape in Web3 encompassing community management, user growth, content and research, ecosystem business development (BD), and governance operations.","points":[["Start here","Select a functional focus: align strengths in communication, writing, data analysis, or negotiation with Community, Content/Growth, Research, or BD."],["Think of it as","If engineers build the open-source locomotive, content researchers write the passenger guidebooks, community managers serve as conductors keeping the carriages safe from pickpockets, and BD managers negotiate track connections with neighboring stations so the train can reach new cities."],["Common mistake","Equating Web3 marketing with spamming hype slogans or buying bot followers rather than educating users and driving real product retention."]]},"t-224":{"title":"On-Chain Derivatives","line":"Derivatives protocols deployed on smart contracts or app-chains that primarily offer perpetual futures with self-custodial margin and transparent on-chain liquidation rules.","points":[["Start here","Identify the execution model: determine whether the protocol uses an order book on a rollup/app-chain or an oracle-priced counterparty liquidity pool."],["Think of it as","A centralized exchange is like handing your chips to a closed casino cashier; an on-chain order book is like locking chips in a glass vault while an electronic board matches buyers and sellers; and an LP-pool protocol is like a public syndicate vault that takes the opposite side of every trader's bet—earning fees when traders lose, but paying out of its own capital when traders win big in a one-way trend."],["Common mistake","Assuming derivatives LP vaults only collect risk-free trading fees—LPs act as the counterparty to traders and suffer principal losses when traders profit in strong trends."]]},"t-225":{"title":"Yield Aggregators & Restaking","line":"Yield aggregators are smart contract vaults that automate reward harvesting and compounding across DeFi protocols, while restaking reuses staked assets to secure additional middleware networks for stacked yields.","points":[["Start here","Trace the underlying yield source: distinguish organic consensus rewards and swap fees from inflationary token emissions."],["Think of it as","A yield aggregator is like neighbors pooling funds to hire an automated property manager who collects rent and reinvests it to save everyone individual trip costs; restaking is like pledging the same house deed as collateral to guarantee three separate business contracts—you collect three guarantee fees in calm times, but a single default in any of the three businesses puts your house on the chopping block."],["Common mistake","Chasing triple-digit advertised APYs funded by hyper-inflationary reward tokens whose price collapse erodes returns."]]},"t-226":{"title":"Aggregators & Launchpads","line":"DEX aggregators are routing protocols that split orders across multiple liquidity venues to minimize slippage and execution costs, while Launchpads (IDOs) facilitate early public token sales and fundraising on-chain.","points":[["Start here","Compare net output after gas: verify that a multi-hop aggregator route still beats a direct single-pool swap after accounting for higher gas usage."],["Think of it as","A DEX aggregator acts like a flight meta-search engine that owns no airplanes but splits a group booking across three airlines to secure the lowest total fare; a Launchpad is like a store requiring you to buy and lock an expensive VIP membership card just to enter a raffle for a discounted item—if the VIP card itself plunges in value or the item is quickly mass-produced, winning the raffle can still leave you at a net loss."],["Common mistake","Assuming multi-pool splitting always saves money on tiny swaps—extra multi-hop gas fees on Mainnet can exceed the tiny slippage saved."]]},"t-227":{"title":"Concentrated Liquidity & Liquidity Management","line":"An automated market maker mechanism allowing liquidity providers (LPs) to allocate capital within custom price ranges (ticks) for higher capital efficiency, alongside active range-management strategies.","points":[["Start here","Match range width to pair volatility: use tight ticks for correlated pairs (like stablecoins) and wider ranges for volatile pairs."],["Think of it as","Full-range liquidity is like setting up water stands every mile along a 500-mile highway where most stands sit empty; concentrated liquidity packs all your inventory right at the busiest rest stop—earning far more sales per dollar of stock, until traffic detours onto another road (price leaves your range), leaving your stand with zero customers unless you pay to haul everything to the new route."],["Common mistake","Setting ultra-narrow ranges on volatile tokens for high headline APRs—prices quickly exit the range, halting fees and magnifying impermanent loss."]]},"t-228":{"title":"GameFi: On-Chain Game Economies","line":"The integration of blockchain asset ownership and decentralized finance mechanics into video games, enabling in-game items, characters, and currencies to be held and traded on-chain.","points":[["Start here","Evaluate token sinks: check whether token burning comes from genuine entertainment consumption or merely minting more yield-bearing NFTs."],["Think of it as","An early P2E economy is like a farm where visitors buy hens to lay eggs, and the main use for eggs is hatching more hens to sell to new visitors: as long as a line of new buyers forms outside, everyone appears profitable—but the moment new visitor growth stalls, everyone dumps eggs at the market simultaneously, crashing the price of both eggs and hens."],["Common mistake","Mistaking new entrants' NFT purchases for sustainable external revenue when nearly all participants are playing solely to extract cash."]]},"t-229":{"title":"SocialFi: Tokenising Social Graphs","line":"The combination of decentralized identity, on-chain social graphs, and tokenomics to give users ownership over their social connections and monetize content or influence.","points":[["Start here","Distinguish protocol graphs from speculative apps: separate portable social graph infrastructure from short-term bonding-curve trading games."],["Think of it as","Web2 social media is like renting a booth in a mall where the landlord owns your customer list and locks it away if you leave; a decentralized social graph lets you carry your own portable customer Rolodex to any storefront; and bonding-curve creator keys act like fan-club passes whose price ratchets up with every buyer—allowing automated scalper bots to buy the first ten passes in milliseconds and dump them on genuine fans at the top."],["Common mistake","Confusing a steep bonding-curve paper price with deep market liquidity—when early holders exit, the curve slides down just as fast."]]},"t-230":{"title":"DAOs & On-Chain Governance","line":"Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs), coordination structures that manage shared treasuries and protocol parameters through smart contracts, token voting, timelocks, and multisigs.","points":[["Start here","Trace the governance lifecycle: verify whether proposals follow a structured RFC forum thread, off-chain Snapshot poll, and binding on-chain execution."],["Think of it as","DAO governance works like a homeowner association managing a shared reserve fund by square footage: forum threads are neighborhood discussions, Snapshot polls are free mobile surveys, on-chain votes are binding payment orders, and a Timelock is a rule requiring any approved withdrawal to sit on a public bulletin board for 48 hours before the bank releases the money—giving residents time to pull their funds or veto the action if a coalition tries to siphon the reserve."],["Common mistake","Assuming every project calling itself a DAO is decentralized—many rely on non-binding polls while founders hold unrestricted multisig keys without a Timelock."]]},"t-231":{"title":"Prediction Markets & InfoFi","line":"Information Finance (InfoFi), a sector using on-chain prediction markets, attention incentives, and reputation staking to aggregate, price, and verify real-world event probabilities and information.","points":[["Start here","Read the exact resolution rules: verify the designated official data source, cutoff timestamp, and edge-case definitions before trading."],["Think of it as","An internet poll is costless talk, whereas a prediction market is a binary voucher that pays $1.00 if an event happens and $0.00 if it does not: if traders pay $0.70 for 'Rain Tomorrow' and $0.30 for 'No Rain,' that $0.70 price acts like a thermometer reading a 70% market-implied chance of rain—yet if the pool holds only $10, one person spending $5 can artificially spike the reading to 90%, and if sleet falls instead of rain, the payout depends on how the oracle referees vote."],["Common mistake","Treating the price in an illiquid prediction market as an objective scientific probability when a single whale can skew thin order books."]]},"i-1":{"title":"Consumer Price Index (CPI)","line":"Tracks prices paid by urban US consumers for a basket including housing, food, transport and services.","points":[["How to read it","In Table 1 find All items. Read the seasonally adjusted one-month change, then the 12-month change and shelter/energy components."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","A falling annual rate is not falling prices; an unusually high comparison month can lower the annual rate."]]},"i-2":{"title":"Core CPI","line":"CPI excluding food and energy helps reveal persistent price pressure behind volatile components.","points":[["How to read it","Find All items less food and energy in CPI Table 1; inspect shelter, medical care and goods components."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","Core CPI is not the formal measure of the Fed’s 2% inflation objective; that objective refers to overall PCE inflation."]]},"i-3":{"title":"PCE Price Index","line":"Measures prices associated with household consumption, with coverage and weights differing from CPI, including some healthcare paid on households’ behalf.","points":[["How to read it","Open Personal Income and Outlays and locate PCE price index in the price tables, not real PCE; distinguish monthly from annual changes."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","PCE prices and the dollar value of spending are different; spending reflects both prices and quantities."]]},"i-4":{"title":"Core PCE Price Index","line":"PCE prices excluding food and energy are used to assess persistence.","points":[["How to read it","Locate PCE excluding food and energy in BEA’s price table; record the latest monthly change, revised prior month and annual change."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","If monthly and annual signals differ, examine base effects and revisions; multiplying one month by 12 is not an exact compounded annual rate."]]},"i-5":{"title":"Producer Price Index (PPI), Final Demand","line":"Tracks selling prices received by domestic producers.","points":[["How to read it","In PPI Table 1 find Final demand, then final-demand goods and services; intermediate demand is a different stage."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","Producer inflation does not pass into CPI one-for-one; firms can absorb margins, switch suppliers or delay repricing."]]},"i-6":{"title":"Core PPI: Final Demand Less Foods and Energy","line":"Here core means final demand less foods and energy.","points":[["How to read it","Find Final demand less foods and energy. The series additionally excluding trade services is different and must not be substituted silently."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","Calendars may use different definitions of core PPI; match the exact series before comparing actual and forecast."]]},"i-7":{"title":"Import Price Index","line":"Tracks dollar prices of US imports of goods and selected services, helping identify external cost pressure.","points":[["How to read it","Open Import and Export Price Indexes; read All imports and Imports excluding fuels, noting the unadjusted basis."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","Import price indexes generally exclude tariffs; they are not the importer’s complete tariff-inclusive cost bill."]]},"i-8":{"title":"Export Price Index","line":"Tracks dollar prices of US exports of goods and selected services.","points":[["How to read it","In the export price table of the international price release find All commodities, then Agricultural and Nonagricultural commodities."],["How it reaches markets","Export prices first affect exporters’ receipts and overseas buyers’ purchasing power, but volumes also determine revenue."],["Common mistake","This is not the trade balance or total export value; mix, the dollar and global supply and demand all matter."]]},"i-9":{"title":"Employment Cost Index (ECI)","line":"Measures changes in hourly employer compensation, including wages and benefits, while controlling for shifts in the mix of jobs.","points":[["How to read it","Locate total compensation for civilian workers, then wages and salaries and benefits separately."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","A 0.8% quarterly change is not a 0.8% annual change; productivity gains can offset higher compensation per hour."]]},"i-10":{"title":"University of Michigan Inflation Expectations","line":"Surveys how much consumers expect prices to rise annually; expectations are not measured current inflation.","points":[["How to read it","Find year-ahead and long-run inflation expectations; identify whether the release is preliminary or final."],["How it reaches markets","The number affects prices through expectations: inflation pressure changes the expected Fed path, which can change Treasury yields and borrowing costs."],["Common mistake","Consumer expectations are not central-bank forecasts; sampling uncertainty and preliminary-to-final changes matter."]]},"i-11":{"title":"Nonfarm Payroll Employment","line":"Counts jobs on nonfarm establishment payrolls.","points":[["How to read it","In Employment Situation locate total nonfarm payroll employment; read Table B-1’s latest change and revisions to the prior two months."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","A person with two payroll jobs can be counted twice; establishment jobs differ from employed people in the household survey."]]},"i-12":{"title":"Unemployment Rate (U-3)","line":"The unemployed share of the labor force: people without work who are available and meet active-search criteria, rather than a share of all residents.","points":[["How to read it","Find Unemployment rate in Table A-1; also examine civilian labor force and the employment-population ratio."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","People who stop searching generally leave the labor force; a lower U-3 rate can therefore accompany a weaker labor market."]]},"i-13":{"title":"Labor Force Participation Rate","line":"The labor force as a share of the civilian noninstitutional population aged 16 and over: how many people are working or seeking work.","points":[["How to read it","Find Participation rate in Table A-1; look up the prime-age (25–54) rate in the BLS database or FRED too so retirement-related aging is not mistaken entirely for cyclical weakness."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","A 0.2 percentage-point change is not 0.2% growth; schooling, retirement and caregiving affect participation."]]},"i-14":{"title":"Average Hourly Earnings","line":"Average gross hourly earnings of private nonfarm employees.","points":[["How to read it","In Table B-3 select all employees, total private; the production and nonsupervisory employee series is a different measure."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","This is nominal gross pay; purchasing power requires a price adjustment, not merely a dollar wage increase."]]},"i-15":{"title":"Average Weekly Hours","line":"Average weekly hours for private nonfarm employees.","points":[["How to read it","In Table B-2 find average weekly hours, all employees, total private; inspect manufacturing overtime as a cross-check."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","Weather, strikes and industry mix affect monthly hours; a 0.2-hour decline alone does not establish recession."]]},"i-16":{"title":"Initial Unemployment Insurance Claims","line":"New applications for unemployment insurance during a week, a timely indicator of layoff pressure but not a count of all newly unemployed people.","points":[["How to read it","In DOL’s release find advance and revised initial claims and the four-week moving average; note the week-ending dates."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","A claim is not an approved benefit; uninsured workers and people who do not apply are not fully captured."]]},"i-17":{"title":"Continuing Unemployment Insurance Claims","line":"Continuing insured claims help assess whether workers remain on unemployment benefits and how easily they may be finding new jobs.","points":[["How to read it","Find insured unemployment or continuing claims; its reference week generally lags initial claims by an additional week."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","Benefit exhaustion can reduce continuing claims even when people remain jobless; the series is not total unemployment."]]},"i-18":{"title":"JOLTS Job Openings","line":"Positions open on the final business day of the month, available to start within 30 days and actively being recruited for; demand, not completed hiring.","points":[["How to read it","Read total nonfarm openings in JOLTS Table 1, then compare hires and layoffs to distinguish reduced recruitment from active job cuts."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","Openings are not payroll gains; vacancies do not guarantee that every advertised position will be filled."]]},"i-19":{"title":"JOLTS Quits Rate","line":"Voluntary quits as a percentage of employment, often used to gauge willingness to change jobs rather than layoffs.","points":[["How to read it","Find the total nonfarm quits rate, not only quits levels, and compare it with the hires rate."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","Quits have a specific definition; retirement and transfers are not all quits, and quits must not be confused with layoffs."]]},"i-20":{"title":"ADP National Employment Report","line":"An ADP estimate of changes in private employment using payroll data.","points":[["How to read it","Open ADP’s report and read private employment by industry and establishment size; keep Pay Insights separate from job counts."],["How it reaches markets","Jobs and pay support household spending and company revenue, while a tight labor market may sustain wage and service inflation and delay Fed easing."],["Common mistake","The redesigned 2022 ADP report is not intended to forecast BLS payrolls; methodology changes matter across versions."]]},"i-21":{"title":"Real Gross Domestic Product (GDP)","line":"Real GDP measures final goods and services produced within the US, adjusted for price changes: an output-side national scorecard.","points":[["How to read it","In the latest GDP release find real GDP; read growth in Table 1 and contributions from consumption, investment, net exports and government."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","A 3% annualized rate is not 3% actual growth during the quarter; fewer imports can lift arithmetic GDP without stronger domestic demand."]]},"i-22":{"title":"Real Gross Domestic Income (GDI)","line":"Adds income earned from production, such as compensation and profits.","points":[["How to read it","Find gross domestic income in the GDP release or NIPA Table 1.17.1; select real rather than current-dollar measures."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","GDI is not household disposable income; it includes business production income and can be available later than advance GDP."]]},"i-23":{"title":"ISM Manufacturing PMI","line":"A diffusion survey of manufacturing purchasing managers covering orders, production, employment and related conditions; 50 marks no change for the surveyed sector.","points":[["How to read it","Read the manufacturing headline, then new orders, production, employment, prices and supplier deliveries."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","50 is not an automatic recession threshold for the whole US economy; slower deliveries can reflect supply disruption."]]},"i-24":{"title":"ISM Services PMI","line":"Surveys service-sector purchasing managers on activity, orders, employment and deliveries, informing broad demand and service-price pressure.","points":[["How to read it","Read services PMI alongside business activity, new orders, employment and prices."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","ISM’s composite services headline is not its business activity subindex and is not identical to S&P’s services headline."]]},"i-25":{"title":"S&P Global US Manufacturing PMI","line":"S&P Global’s monthly US manufacturing survey.","points":[["How to read it","Open S&P PMI releases, choose United States and Manufacturing PMI; identify flash versus final before reading orders, output and price commentary."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","S&P and ISM can diverge legitimately; never compare one provider’s forecast with the other’s actual."]]},"i-26":{"title":"S&P Global US Services PMI Business Activity Index","line":"The S&P US services headline is a business activity index, not a simple average of every service-sector subindex.","points":[["How to read it","Find Services PMI Business Activity Index, distinguish it from Composite Output Index, then examine new business and selling prices."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","Composite PMI combines sectors and is not the services index; missing expectations does not necessarily mean output fell."]]},"i-27":{"title":"Industrial Production Index","line":"Measures real output from manufacturing, mining and utilities, rather than the full service-heavy US economy.","points":[["How to read it","In the Fed’s G.17 release read industrial production and separate manufacturing, mining and utilities, with monthly changes and revisions."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","Weather can swing utilities; industrial production is neither factory orders nor the whole of GDP."]]},"i-28":{"title":"Capacity Utilization","line":"Industrial output relative to sustainable capacity: making 78 units out of a sustainable 100 means roughly 78% utilization.","points":[["How to read it","Find total-industry capacity utilization in G.17 and compare it with its long-run average, manufacturing utilization and output."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","High utilization does not guarantee profits; costs, maintenance and normal industry levels differ."]]},"i-29":{"title":"Durable Goods Orders","line":"New orders for manufactured goods generally expected to last at least three years, including machinery, vehicles and aircraft.","points":[["How to read it","Open the M3 advance durable goods report; read new orders and the measure excluding transportation."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","Orders can be canceled or delayed and are neither shipments nor profits; nominal growth can also reflect prices."]]},"i-30":{"title":"Core Capital Goods Orders","line":"Usually nondefense capital goods orders excluding aircraft, used to assess business equipment demand with less defense and aircraft volatility.","points":[["How to read it","In M3 locate new orders for nondefense capital goods excluding aircraft; shipments are a separate series."],["How it reaches markets","Activity changes expected sales and profits, the earnings channel for stocks."],["Common mistake","Core does not remove every volatile item; orders are not interchangeable with shipment inputs used to estimate equipment investment."]]},"i-31":{"title":"Advance Retail Sales","line":"Dollar sales at retailers and food services, mainly informing goods spending rather than the full range of household services.","points":[["How to read it","Open Advance Monthly Sales on Census Retail; read the total, the measure excluding motor vehicles, and industry detail."],["How it reaches markets","Household spending becomes business revenue, and sustainable real income supports future demand, affecting equity earnings expectations."],["Common mistake","The headline is not price-adjusted; online retail is a channel, not all nonphysical or service spending."]]},"i-32":{"title":"Retail Sales Control Group","line":"A common analytical grouping excludes autos, gasoline, building materials and food services to inform estimates of underlying goods consumption.","points":[["How to read it","Use the official advance table to verify the total and four excluded categories; check that a calendar’s control-group definition matches."],["How it reaches markets","Household spending becomes business revenue, and sustainable real income supports future demand, affecting equity earnings expectations."],["Common mistake","The control group is neither real consumption nor all services; some calendars use core retail to mean ex-autos only."]]},"i-33":{"title":"Real Personal Consumption Expenditures","line":"Price-adjusted household consumption of goods and services, used to assess the volume of demand rather than dollar spending alone.","points":[["How to read it","In Personal Income and Outlays find real PCE and separate goods from services; PCE price index measures prices instead."],["How it reaches markets","Household spending becomes business revenue, and sustainable real income supports future demand, affecting equity earnings expectations."],["Common mistake","Chained-dollar components are not always additive; strong spending can also increase expected interest rates."]]},"i-34":{"title":"Personal Income","line":"Income received by persons from earnings, property and transfers, helping assess whether spending has support beyond borrowing.","points":[["How to read it","Find personal income in the BEA release and inspect compensation and current transfer receipts by source."],["How it reaches markets","Household spending becomes business revenue, and sustainable real income supports future demand, affecting equity earnings expectations."],["Common mistake","Aggregate income does not mean everyone gets the same rise; distribution and inflation matter."]]},"i-35":{"title":"Disposable Personal Income (DPI)","line":"Personal income after personal current taxes, available for spending or saving; disposable does not mean after rent and other living expenses.","points":[["How to read it","Find disposable personal income and real DPI, check whether the series is per capita, and compare the personal saving rate."],["How it reaches markets","Household spending becomes business revenue, and sustainable real income supports future demand, affecting equity earnings expectations."],["Common mistake","DPI is not a brokerage cash balance; tax changes can alter DPI without a change in gross wages."]]},"i-36":{"title":"Personal Saving Rate","line":"The Personal Saving Rate, published monthly by the U.S. Bureau of Economic Analysis (BEA) within the Personal Income and Outlays report, measures the percentage of disposable personal income (after-tax income) remaining after households pay for personal consumption expenditures, non-mortgage interest, and current transfer payments. It captures the monthly flow ratio of unspent income across the entire U.S. household sector rather than cumulative cash sitting in bank accounts. Beginners often misread it as bank deposit growth or assume stock and home price appreciation boosts the saving rate.","points":[["How to read it","Decompose income versus outlays: read Disposable Personal Income (DPI) and Personal Consumption Expenditures (PCE) side by side to see whether saving rose from stronger income or falling spending."],["How it reaches markets","The transmission is indirect: changes in the saving rate shape expectations for future consumer spending durability → altering corporate revenue and GDP forecasts → influencing how the Federal Reserve weighs demand strength against inflation → moving Treasury yields, dollar liquidity, and broad risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Confusing the flow saving rate with total bank account balances or assuming rising stock and home prices count as personal saving income."]]},"i-37":{"title":"Conference Board Consumer Confidence Index","line":"The Conference Board Consumer Confidence Index, published monthly by The Conference Board, surveys U.S. households on their assessment of current business and job conditions as well as their expectations for business, employment, and income over the next six months.","points":[["How to read it","Compare actual, consensus, and revised prior: check how many index points the headline beat or missed forecasts and check prior-month revisions."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: shifts in household job and income confidence → alter expectations for discretionary spending and labor-market slack → reshape market probabilities of a soft landing versus a recession → move Federal Reserve rate expectations and Treasury yields, which in turn affect equity valuations and crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Misreading 105 index points as 105% optimism, or comparing the Conference Board's ~100 level directly against the University of Michigan's ~70 level."]]},"i-38":{"title":"University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index","line":"The University of Michigan Consumer Sentiment Index, published twice a month (Preliminary mid-month and Final at month-end) by the University of Michigan Surveys of Consumers, gauges U.S. households' views on their personal finances, general business conditions, and big-ticket buying conditions, alongside closely watched 1-year and 5–10-year inflation expectations.","points":[["How to read it","Read both sentiment and inflation expectations: check the three sentiment index-point lines first, then read the 1-year and 5–10-year inflation expectation percentages."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission runs through two distinct channels: first, household financial confidence shapes big-ticket spending intentions and corporate earnings expectations; second—and often more important for markets—household inflation expectations are monitored by the Federal Reserve to judge whether inflation psychology remains anchored."],["Common mistake","Confusing index points with percentage points, or focusing solely on the sentiment headline while ignoring the market-moving 1-year and 5–10-year inflation expectations."]]},"i-39":{"title":"Consumer Credit G.19","line":"Consumer Credit (the G.19 statistical release), published monthly by the Federal Reserve Board about five weeks after the reference month, measures outstanding U.S. household debt that is not secured by real estate, split into Revolving credit (mainly credit cards) and Nonrevolving credit (such as auto loans and student loans).","points":[["How to read it","Split Revolving from Nonrevolving: compare credit-card borrowing (Revolving) against auto and student loan borrowing (Nonrevolving)."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect and lagging: trends in consumer borrowing → reveal household willingness to finance purchases and bank willingness to extend credit → shape expectations for retail revenues and bank loan losses → feed into Federal Reserve assessments of financial conditions, bond yields, and equity/crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Treating G.19 as total household debt when it excludes residential mortgages (which make up the largest share of household liabilities)."]]},"i-40":{"title":"Household Loan Delinquency Rates","line":"Household Loan Delinquency Rates, published quarterly by the Federal Reserve Board (from commercial bank regulatory filings) and supplemented by the New York Fed's Quarterly Report on Household Debt and Credit, measure the percentage of loan balances in credit cards, auto loans, consumer loans, and residential mortgages that are past due.","points":[["How to read it","Distinguish Delinquency from Charge-offs and Transitions: verify whether you are reading the stock Delinquency Rate, the Charge-off Rate, or the New York Fed's quarterly Transition Rate."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect and medium-term: rising delinquency rates → signal mounting household debt servicing strain → force households to curb discretionary spending while prompting banks to raise loan-loss provisions and tighten lending standards → weigh on corporate earnings expectations and credit availability → pressure equity valuations (especially banks and consumer cyclicals) and broad crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Assuming a lower delinquency rate always means healthier borrowers when writing off bad loans into charge-offs (shrinking the numerator) or rapid new lending (growing the denominator) can artificially lower the ratio."]]},"i-41":{"title":"Housing Starts","line":"Housing Starts, published monthly by the U.S. Census Bureau and the Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) in the New Residential Construction report, count the number of privately owned new housing units where excavation (groundbreaking) actually began during the reference month. It measures physical construction starts rather than pre-construction permits or completed home sales. Beginners often misread a headline '1.40 million Seasonally Adjusted Annual Rate' as meaning 1.40 million homes broke ground in that single month.","points":[["How to read it","Compare actual, consensus, and revisions: check the headline SAAR level and monthly percentage change against forecasts and prior-month revisions, noting wide confidence intervals."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: changes in housing starts → drive construction employment, lumber/copper demand, and future appliance purchases → feed directly into the Residential Fixed Investment component of GDP → influence how the Federal Reserve judges the bite of interest rates on the real economy → move Treasury yields and broad risk sentiment across equities and crypto."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a 1.40 million annualized rate (SAAR) for 1.40 million homes started in a single month (actual unadjusted monthly starts are roughly 100k–130k)."]]},"i-42":{"title":"Building Permits","line":"Building Permits, published monthly by the U.S. Census Bureau and HUD alongside Housing Starts in the New Residential Construction report, count the number of privately owned new housing units authorized by building permits in permit-issuing jurisdictions during the reference month. They measure pre-construction regulatory approvals and builder intentions—one step ahead of Housing Starts (Lesson 41)—rather than active excavations or finished homes. Beginners often assume a permit means construction has already begun or overlook that non-permit rural areas are excluded from permit counts.","points":[["How to read it","Compare actual, consensus, and revisions: compare the headline SAAR figure against consensus forecasts and prior revisions, noting that permit data have much smaller sampling error than starts."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect and forward-looking: changes in building permits → foreshadow construction employment, materials orders, and housing investment 1 to 3 months ahead (making Building Permits one of the 10 components of the Conference Board's Leading Economic Index) → shift expectations for GDP growth and cyclical turning points → influence Treasury yields and Federal Reserve rate expectations → filter into equity valuations and crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Treating an approved Building Permit as a completed or actively started home when projects can sit unstarted for months or be canceled."]]},"i-43":{"title":"Existing-Home Sales","line":"Existing-Home Sales, published monthly by the National Association of Realtors (NAR), count the number of previously owned U.S. homes (single-family homes, townhomes, condominiums, and co-ops) where sales transactions officially closed (title transferred) during the reference month, alongside active inventory and median sales prices.","points":[["How to read it","Compare SAAR sales against consensus and prior: read the headline annualized sales pace and monthly percentage change against forecasts."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: existing-home turnover → drives real-estate commissions, mortgage originations, moving services, and home-improvement/appliance spending, while showing how mortgage rates from 30–60 days ago constrained household mobility → shapes expectations for consumer spending and economic resilience → influences Treasury yields and risk appetite across equities and crypto."],["Common mistake","Comparing closed Existing-Home Sales directly with contract-based New Home Sales (Lesson 44) in the same month without accounting for the 1-to-2-month closing lag."]]},"i-44":{"title":"New Residential Sales","line":"New Residential Sales (commonly called New Home Sales), published monthly by the U.S. Census Bureau and HUD, count the number of newly built privately owned single-family homes sold at the stage of signing a sales contract or accepting a buyer deposit, along with for-sale inventory and sale prices. Although new homes represent only roughly 10%–15% of total U.S. residential transactions, counting sales at contract signing makes this series 1 to 2 months timelier than closing-based Existing-Home Sales (Lesson 43). Beginners often overlook that it covers only single-family houses (excluding condos) and carries wide monthly sampling error.","points":[["How to read it","Check actual, consensus, revisions, and confidence intervals: compare the headline against forecasts and revisions to the prior three months, noting the wide ±12% to ±18% 90% confidence interval."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: shifts in new-home contract sales and builder inventory → directly determine homebuilder cash flow, land/construction plans, and whether builders must offer price cuts or mortgage-rate buydowns to move inventory → drive future Housing Starts (Lesson 41), construction jobs, and materials demand → shape GDP growth and rate expectations → influence equities and crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Reacting to a single month's +8% or -8% headline move without checking the massive ±12%–18% sampling error margin and the revisions to the prior three months."]]},"i-45":{"title":"Pending Home Sales Index","line":"The Pending Home Sales Index (PHS), published monthly by the National Association of Realtors (NAR), tracks existing-home transactions where a purchase contract has been signed during the reference month but the deal has not yet closed.","points":[["How to read it","Focus on the m/m % change versus consensus: compare the seasonally adjusted monthly percentage change against economic calendar forecasts and prior revisions."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect and leading: current mortgage rates and listing availability → immediately impact buyers' willingness to sign contracts (captured in Pending Home Sales) → foreshadow completed Existing-Home Sales, broker fees, mortgage originations, and home-related retail spending 1 to 2 months ahead → shape market views on consumer and housing resilience → influence Treasury yields and broad equity/crypto risk sentiment."],["Common mistake","Misreading an index level of 84 as 840,000 homes sold, or assuming an index below 100 means house prices have fallen below 2001 levels."]]},"i-46":{"title":"S&P Cotality Case-Shiller Home Price Indices","line":"The S&P Cotality (formerly CoreLogic) Case-Shiller Home Price Indices, published monthly by S&P Dow Jones Indices, use the repeat-sales methodology developed by Karl Case and Robert Shiller to track price changes across matched sales of the exact same single-family homes over time.","points":[["How to read it","Separate National from 20-City Composite: locate both the U.S. National Index and the 20-City Composite and match the exact series used by your consensus calendar."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect and medium-term: repeat-sales home price trends → alter household housing wealth (the wealth effect) and home-equity borrowing capacity while affecting mortgage collateral cushions → filter with a 12-to-18-month lag into housing rent inflation (Shelter / Owners' Equivalent Rent) inside CPI and PCE → shape Federal Reserve views on sticky service inflation and the path of interest rates → influence Treasury yields, equities, and crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Forgetting the triple lag (1–2 months from contract to closing, a 3-month moving average, and a 2-month publication delay) and treating the late-May release as live May pricing."]]},"i-47":{"title":"FHFA House Price Index","line":"The FHFA House Price Index (FHFA HPI), published monthly and quarterly by the Federal Housing Finance Agency, uses a repeat-sales methodology to track single-family home price changes across mortgages acquired or assured by Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac.","points":[["How to read it","Compare actual, consensus, and prior revisions: evaluate the monthly m/m % change alongside any revision to the prior month's growth rate."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: price trends in conforming mortgage-backed homes → determine Loan-to-Value (LTV) cushions across Fannie/Freddie MBS pools and commercial banks while shaping middle-class household equity → influence consumer credit capacity and long-run shelter inflation expectations → feed into Federal Reserve policy assessments, MBS spreads, Treasury yields, and broad equity/crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Forgetting that the monthly FHFA Purchase-Only index excludes all-cash buyers and jumbo mortgages above conforming limits."]]},"i-48":{"title":"MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications","line":"The MBA Weekly Mortgage Applications Survey, published every Wednesday morning by the Mortgage Bankers Association, tracks weekly changes in the volume of single-family mortgage loan applications submitted to U.S. commercial banks, thrifts, and mortgage bankers during the week ended the prior Friday.","points":[["How to read it","Separate Purchase from Refinance immediately: read the seasonally adjusted Purchase Index w/w % change separately from the Refinance Index w/w % change rather than relying on the Market Composite."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: weekly moves in Treasury yields and mortgage rates → immediately alter household refinancing and home-purchase loan applications → lead Pending Home Sales (Lesson 45) and New Home Sales (Lesson 44) by several weeks while refinancing savings free up monthly household cash flow → inform market views on how tightly interest rates are biting housing → filter into bond yields, housing equities, and broad stock/crypto risk sentiment."],["Common mistake","Reading a +10% jump in the headline Market Composite Index as a 10% surge in homebuyers without checking whether Refinance or Purchase drove the move."]]},"i-49":{"title":"Freddie Mac Primary Mortgage Market Survey PMMS","line":"The Freddie Mac Primary Mortgage Market Survey (PMMS), published every Thursday at noon Eastern by Freddie Mac, reports the weekly U.S. national average interest rate on the benchmark 30-Year Fixed-Rate Mortgage (and 15-Year Fixed-Rate Mortgage).","points":[["How to read it","Calculate the basis-point change alongside the 10-Year Treasury: compare the weekly mortgage rate change (in bps) with how the 10-year Treasury yield moved from the prior Thursday to Wednesday."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: inflation and monetary policy expectations move the 10-year Treasury yield and Mortgage-Backed Securities (MBS) spreads → setting the 30-year mortgage rate → directly altering monthly principal-and-interest payments (housing affordability) and homeowner mobility → driving home sales, construction starts, and durable-goods spending → feeding back into GDP growth, bond yields, and equity/crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Assuming a Federal Reserve rate cut automatically lowers 30-year mortgage rates by the same amount—if long-term 10-year Treasury yields rise after a Fed cut, mortgage rates can actually go up."]]},"i-50":{"title":"NAHB/Wells Fargo Housing Market Index HMI","line":"The NAHB/Wells Fargo Housing Market Index (HMI), published monthly (typically mid-month) by the National Association of Home Builders, surveys U.S. single-family homebuilders on their ratings of current new-home sales, sales expectations over the next six months, and traffic of prospective buyers.","points":[["How to read it","Compare actual, consensus, and prior: check how many index points HMI beat or missed forecasts and track its multi-month direction."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect and leading: shifts in mortgage rates, construction costs, and model-home foot traffic → immediately reshape single-family builder confidence (captured in HMI) → drive builders' decisions to pull Building Permits (Lesson 42), break ground on Housing Starts (Lesson 41), and order materials/labor → alter market forecasts for residential GDP investment and cyclical momentum → influence Treasury yields, homebuilder/retail equities, and broad stock/crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Interpreting a rise from 45 to 49 as 'widespread builder optimism' or a '4% increase in home sales' when any reading below 50 still means negative responses outnumber positive ones."]]},"i-51":{"title":"Federal Reserve Balance Sheet","line":"The Federal Reserve Balance Sheet (officially the H.4.1 statistical release, 'Factors Affecting Reserve Balances'), published every Thursday at 4:30 p.m.","points":[["How to read it","Decompose Assets into Securities versus Loans: check whether a change in Total Assets came from scheduled Treasury/MBS runoff (QT) or a spike in Discount Window / emergency loans."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: changes in the Fed's securities holdings and loans, combined with shifts between TGA and ON RRP on the liability side → determine the supply of commercial bank Reserve Balances and overnight funding conditions → influence Treasury term premia, repo rates (SOFR), and financial conditions → affect equity discount rates and macro liquidity appetite for crypto."],["Common mistake","Looking only at a rise in Total Assets and shouting 'QE is back!' without checking whether the increase came from emergency bank-rescue loans rather than bond purchases."]]},"i-52":{"title":"Reserve Balances of Depository Institutions","line":"Reserve Balances of Depository Institutions (commonly called Bank Reserves), published weekly in the Federal Reserve's H.4.1 release and monthly in the H.6 release, measure the total dollar balances that commercial banks and other depository institutions hold in their master accounts at Federal Reserve Banks.","points":[["How to read it","Decompose the balance-sheet identity: compare weekly changes in Reserves against changes in Fed Securities (QT), the TGA (Lesson 54), and ON RRP (Lesson 53) to see what drove the move."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is indirect: the total level and distribution of bank reserves across large and small banks → govern cash availability in interbank and Treasury repo funding markets → if reserves drop from 'ample' toward 'scarce', banks hoard liquidity and overnight repo rates (like SOFR) spike above the Fed's IORB rate (as occurred in September 2019) → forcing leveraged funds and dealers to unwind positions and pressuring the Fed to halt QT → directly impacting bond markets, equities, and crypto liquidity."],["Common mistake","Adding commercial bank Reserves at the Fed to household M2 bank deposits as if they were the same pool of money, or thinking retail stock buying directly spends Fed reserves."]]},"i-53":{"title":"Overnight Reverse Repurchase Facility ON RRP","line":"The Overnight Reverse Repurchase Facility (ON RRP), published every U.S. business day around 1:15 p.m.","points":[["How to read it","Read Accepted Amount, Counterparties, and Rate: note the daily dollar balance (in billions), number of participants, and the administered award rate."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission works through balance-sheet plumbing: when the Treasury issues short-term T-bills at yields slightly above the ON RRP rate → money market funds withdraw cash from ON RRP to buy those T-bills → allowing the Treasury to raise cash (or the Fed to run QT) by draining the ON RRP buffer rather than draining commercial Bank Reserves (Lesson 52) → keeping bank reserves ample and repo rates stable → indirectly supporting equity valuations and crypto liquidity conditions."],["Common mistake","Mixing up central bank terminology: at the U.S. Federal Reserve, a Reverse Repo (ON RRP) absorbs cash from counterparties (the opposite of the People's Bank of China's terminology, where a 'reverse repo' injects cash)."]]},"i-54":{"title":"Treasury General Account TGA","line":"The Treasury General Account (TGA) is the U.S. Treasury's official operational cash account held at the Federal Reserve; tax receipts and proceeds from Treasury debt issuance flow into this account, while federal benefit payments, vendor outlays, and maturing debt redemptions flow out. Crucially, the TGA records a point-in-time cash balance (a stock) rather than the daily or monthly federal budget deficit (a flow), nor is it discretionary money that the Federal Reserve can spend on its own.","points":[["How to read it","Cross-check daily tax receipts, federal withdrawals, and net public-debt issuance and redemptions, then compare the current balance against the end-of-quarter cash target announced in the Treasury Quarterly Refunding documents."],["How it reaches markets","Within the Federal Reserve's balance-sheet identity, all else equal, a rising TGA balance drains commercial bank reserves while a falling TGA balance adds reserves; however, if money market funds shift cash out of the Overnight Reverse Repo Facility (ON RRP) to purchase newly issued Treasury bills, the drop in ON RRP can cushion or offset the reserve drain from a higher TGA, altering short-term funding tightness and risk appetite across equities and crypto."],["Common mistake","Do not mistake a falling TGA balance for a fiscal surplus or government insolvency, do not equate daily TGA changes with changes in the federal budget deficit, and never overlay the TGA line onto equity or crypto charts in isolation from ON RRP and bank reserves."]]},"i-55":{"title":"M2 Money Stock","line":"M2 Money Stock is a broad monetary aggregate published by the Federal Reserve that sums transaction money immediately available for spending (M1, including currency outside banks, demand deposits, and savings deposits) together with near-money assets easily convertible into cash (small-denomination time deposits under $100,000 and retail money market fund shares).","points":[["How to read it","Break down currency, demand and savings deposits (M1), small time deposits, and retail money funds; remember that the May 2020 savings-deposit reclassification shifted boundaries inside M2 rather than mechanically inflating total M2."],["How it reaches markets","M2 growth reflects broad nominal liquidity held by households and firms: steady expansion can support spending and asset allocation, whereas excessive money growth alongside supply constraints can fuel inflation and force tighter central-bank policy."],["Common mistake","Do not conflate M2 with Federal Reserve balance-sheet assets or bank reserve balances, and remember that the May 2020 savings-deposit reclassification mechanically altered M1 rather than M2; always check seasonal adjustments and historical revisions."]]},"i-56":{"title":"Commercial Bank Deposits H.8","line":"Commercial Bank Deposits in the Federal Reserve's weekly H.8 release track total deposit liabilities owed to households and businesses across commercial banks in the United States, serving as a high-frequency gauge of banking-system funding stability.","points":[["How to read it","Compare deposit breakdowns across large domestically chartered banks, small domestically chartered banks, and foreign-related institutions to determine whether a weekly move reflects system-wide drainage or a flight-to-safety rotation between bank tiers."],["How it reaches markets","Deposits represent banks' primary low-cost funding base: sustained deposit outflows can force banks to pay higher funding rates, tap costlier wholesale borrowing, or curtail lending, tightening credit conditions for the real economy and equities."],["Common mistake","Do not declare a systemic bank run from a single week's unadjusted deposit drop without checking tax payment dates, quarter-end window dressing, and H.8 merger/reclassification footnotes, and do not confuse shifts into money market funds with destroyed money."]]},"i-57":{"title":"Commercial Bank Loans H.8","line":"Commercial Bank Loans and Leases in the Federal Reserve's weekly H.8 release measure the total stock of outstanding credit extended by commercial banks that has not yet been repaid.","points":[["How to read it","Decompose the change across C&I, commercial and residential real estate, and consumer credit categories, and check the release footnotes for loan sales, securitizations, charge-offs, or non-bank acquisitions."],["How it reaches markets","Bank credit finances business capital expenditure and household consumption, yet rising loan balances can reflect either healthy expansion or stressed corporations drawing down precautionary revolving credit lines during a crunch; conversely, contracting balances can stem from weak borrower demand under high rates or banks restricting credit supply."],["Common mistake","Do not automatically interpret a sudden spike in C&I loan balances as booming investment—firms often draw down existing credit lines defensively during liquidity scares—and always check footnotes for charge-offs and asset transfers."]]},"i-58":{"title":"Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey SLOOS","line":"The Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey on Bank Lending Practices (SLOOS) is a quarterly qualitative survey conducted by the Federal Reserve that asks senior loan officers at domestic and U.S. branches of foreign commercial banks two distinct sets of questions over the past three months: did your bank tighten or ease credit approval standards, and did borrower demand for loans strengthen or weaken?","points":[["How to read it","Review breakdowns across commercial and industrial (C&I) loans to large/middle-market and small firms, commercial real estate (CRE) loans, residential mortgages, and consumer credit cards and auto loans to see if supply tightening and weak demand align."],["How it reaches markets","SLOOS often leads actual loan growth and default cycles: when a broad majority of banks tighten standards and widen loan spreads, marginal businesses and households face harder refinancing hurdles, curbing future capital spending, hiring, and consumption; meanwhile, plunging loan demand reflects the drag of high interest rates or economic uncertainty."],["Common mistake","Never misinterpret a drop in positive net tightening (e.g., from +30 to +20) as net easing—as long as net tightening is above zero, banks are tightening standards further—and do not confuse qualitative survey percentages with actual loan interest rates or dollar volumes."]]},"i-59":{"title":"Secured Overnight Financing Rate SOFR","line":"The Secured Overnight Financing Rate (SOFR), published jointly by the Federal Reserve Bank of New York and the Office of Financial Research (OFR), is a broad benchmark interest rate measuring the cost of borrowing cash overnight collateralized by U.S. Treasury securities in the repurchase agreement (repo) market. Because it is grounded in over a trillion dollars of actual daily Treasury repo transactions rather than survey quotes, SOFR serves as the primary thermometer for U.S. dollar overnight funding conditions.","points":[["How to read it","Compare daily SOFR and its upper percentiles (75th and 99th) against the Fed's administered rates (ON RRP and IORB) and the prior day's reading to separate routine policy-rate adjustments from genuine repo funding stress."],["How it reaches markets","SOFR underpins trillions of dollars in floating-rate corporate loans, money market instruments, and derivatives: under normal conditions it trades smoothly inside the Federal Reserve's administered rate corridor (near the ON RRP and IORB rates), but when heavy Treasury settlements, scarce bank reserves, or quarter-end dealer balance-sheet constraints create a cash squeeze, unusual SOFR spikes can force leveraged funds and market makers to deleverage, pressuring equities and crypto."],["Common mistake","Do not confuse secured SOFR with the unsecured Effective Federal Funds Rate (EFFR), remember that daily SOFR is a volume-weighted median rather than a simple arithmetic mean, and do not extrapolate a one-day quarter-end spike into an immediate recession."]]},"i-60":{"title":"Discount Window Borrowing Primary Credit","line":"Discount Window Borrowing (focused primarily on Primary Credit in the Federal Reserve's H.4.1 release) measures short-term collateralized loans extended directly by the Federal Reserve to eligible depository institutions against pledged loans or securities.","points":[["How to read it","Separate Primary credit, Secondary credit, Seasonal credit, and any other special liquidity facilities rather than lumping distinct central-bank lending programs into a single number."],["How it reaches markets","Discount window usage carries a dual market signal: on one hand, central-bank collateralized lending prevents banks from dumping securities or calling loans at fire-sale prices during deposit outflows, acting as a systemic stabilizer; on the other hand, an abrupt surge in borrowing outside routine test periods often reveals that certain banks are experiencing deposit flight or losing access to normal wholesale funding, weighing on equity and crypto risk appetite."],["Common mistake","Do not conflate temporary liquidity borrowing with capital insolvency—discount window balances measure collateralized borrowing rather than net capital losses—and do not equate emergency backstop borrowing with proactive quantitative easing (QE)."]]},"i-61":{"title":"U.S. Treasury Auction Results","line":"U.S. Treasury Auction Results record the pricing and allotment details when the U.S. Department of the Treasury sells newly issued marketable debt (including Treasury Bills, Notes, Bonds, TIPS, and Floating Rate Notes) in the primary market. These releases directly reveal the yield and price global institutional and retail investors demand to absorb incoming federal debt supply.","points":[["How to read it","Read the High Yield and Bid-to-Cover ratio, then immediately compare the High Yield against the same-maturity When-Issued (WI) yield at the bidding deadline (typically 1:00 p.m. Eastern): a High Yield above the WI yield produces a positive 'tail' (weaker demand), while a High Yield below WI is a 'stop-through' (stronger demand)."],["How it reaches markets","Treasury auctions test real-money appetite for risk-free duration: weak demand at a benchmark 10-year or 30-year auction (marked by a wide positive tail and heavy primary-dealer takedown) can push secondary Treasury yields higher, raising equity discount rates and tightening crypto funding conditions."],["Common mistake","Never judge an auction by the Bid-to-Cover ratio or the raw High Yield level in isolation; always evaluate the tail/stop-through relative to the deadline When-Issued yield and check the share left with Primary Dealers."]]},"i-62":{"title":"Net Marketable Treasury Borrowing","line":"Net Marketable Treasury Borrowing measures the net new funding the U.S. Treasury raises from the private market over a given period by taking gross issuance of marketable Treasury securities and subtracting the principal of maturing securities repaid during the same window. While gross Treasury issuance runs into tens of trillions of dollars annually, the vast majority simply refinances (rolls over) maturing debt; only net borrowing represents incremental new supply that investors must absorb.","points":[["How to read it","Check the accompanying Assumed End-of-Quarter TGA Cash Balance in the Monday borrowing release, then inspect the Wednesday refunding breakdown between short-term T-Bills and medium-to-long-term coupon auctions."],["How it reaches markets","Both the size and maturity mix (short-term Treasury Bills vs. longer-dated Coupon Notes and Bonds) of net issuance shape bond yields and market liquidity: heavy surprises in long-dated coupon supply can push up term premia and long-term Treasury yields, pressuring equity valuations and crypto leverage; conversely, net issuance skewed toward short-term T-Bills can often be absorbed by money market funds rotating cash out of the Fed's ON RRP facility without directly draining commercial bank reserves."],["Common mistake","Never confuse gross issuance with net borrowing, always distinguish the auction date from the actual cash settlement date, and remember that net borrowing equals the fiscal deficit plus any planned change in the TGA cash balance."]]},"i-63":{"title":"Monthly Treasury Budget Balance","line":"The Monthly Treasury Budget Balance (from the Monthly Treasury Statement, MTS) measures the difference between total federal government outlays (spending) and total receipts (primarily individual and corporate income taxes and social insurance taxes) over a given month or fiscal year: outlays exceeding receipts produce a budget deficit, while receipts exceeding outlays produce a budget surplus.","points":[["How to read it","Read monthly total receipts, total outlays, the monthly surplus or deficit, and the Fiscal Year-to-Date (FYTD, starting October 1) cumulative balance, checking the report notes for calendar timing shifts when the 1st of a month fell on a weekend or holiday."],["How it reaches markets","Fiscal deficits exert two competing forces on markets: from a macro demand standpoint, deficit spending injects income into households and revenues into corporations, supporting earnings during slowdowns; from a financial supply standpoint, large and growing structural deficits require heavier Treasury debt issuance, which can lift inflation expectations, term premia, and long-term Treasury yields, tightening valuation multiples for equities and crypto."],["Common mistake","Do not confuse a budget deficit with a sovereign default, do not mistake an April tax-season monthly surplus for permanent fiscal consolidation, and remember that TGA cash buffers prevent a one-for-one monthly link between the deficit and debt issuance."]]},"i-64":{"title":"Crypto ETF Daily Net Flows","line":"Crypto ETF Daily Net Flows track the net dollar amount of capital entering or leaving spot cryptocurrency exchange-traded funds (such as spot Bitcoin or Ether ETFs) on each U.S. equity trading day through primary-market share creations minus redemptions.","points":[["How to read it","Inspect individual fund columns alongside the aggregate Total column, waiting until all major issuers have reported for the day before citing the Total—never treat unreported blank cells as zero flows."],["How it reaches markets","Because Authorized Participants (APs) creating or redeeming spot ETF shares ultimately trigger purchases or sales of the underlying cryptocurrency, sustained multi-day net inflows across multiple funds provide tangible spot demand; however, hedge funds often buy spot ETFs while simultaneously shorting CME futures in delta-neutral basis trades (capturing yield spreads rather than making a directional bet), and fee-driven rotation between funds can distort single-issuer numbers."],["Common mistake","Do not confuse secondary-market ETF share trading volume with primary-market net creation/redemption flows, do not mistake price-driven AUM gains for net inflows, and never cite an incomplete intraday Total before all major issuers have reported."]]},"i-65":{"title":"Stablecoin Reserve and Attestation Reports","line":"Stablecoin Reserve and Attestation Reports are periodic disclosures published by fiat-backed stablecoin issuers (such as the issuers of USDC or USDT) accompanied by independent third-party accountant attestation reports.","points":[["How to read it","Compare total circulating token liabilities against total reserve assets to verify ≥100% coverage and calculate any excess reserve cushion, then decompose the reserve portfolio into cash deposits, short-term U.S. Treasury Bills, overnight Treasury reverse repos, and any higher-risk or less-liquid loans, commercial paper, or other assets."],["How it reaches markets","Fiat-backed stablecoins serve as the crypto market's primary quote currency, settlement rail, and derivatives collateral: transparent reserves concentrated in highly liquid, short-duration assets (such as short-term U.S. Treasury Bills, overnight reverse repos, and regulated cash deposits) anchor confidence in the $1.00 peg. Conversely, reserve shortfalls, opaque corporate loans, duration mismatches, or custodian bank failures can trigger a secondary-market depeg, redemption runs, and forced deleveraging across crypto markets (though routine clean attestations rarely move broad U.S. equities)."],["Common mistake","Never equate a point-in-time reserve attestation with a full-scope annual financial statement audit, and never look only at the ≥100% headline ratio without inspecting the underlying liquidity, maturity profile, and custodian concentration of the reserve assets."]]},"i-66":{"title":"Federal Reserve FOMC Rate Decision","line":"The Federal Reserve FOMC Rate Decision, announced eight times a year on scheduled Wednesdays at 2:00 p.m.","points":[["How to read it","Read the quarterly SEP and Dot Plot (Mar/Jun/Sep/Dec): check how many basis points the median dot shifted for this year and next year, alongside GDP, unemployment, and Core PCE forecasts."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission is powerful and multi-channel: FOMC changes to the current target range, QT pace, Dot Plot, and statement wording → immediately reprice fed funds futures and 2-year / 10-year Treasury yields → alter corporate borrowing costs, mortgage rates, and the discount rate used to value future equity cash flows → shift interest-rate differentials that drive the U.S. Dollar Index (DXY) → flow through to equities (especially rate-sensitive growth stocks) and crypto leverage/risk appetite. Markets trade the surprise relative to pre-meeting expectations priced into rate futures, not the headline action in isolation."],["Common mistake","Trading off a headline alert ('Fed cuts 25 bps') without checking that the cut was already 100% priced in while the Dot Plot actually removed future cuts."]]},"i-67":{"title":"ECB Monetary Policy Decision","line":"The ECB Monetary Policy Decision, announced eight times a year by the European Central Bank Governing Council on scheduled Thursdays at 14:15 Frankfurt time followed by the President's press conference at 14:45 (with Macroeconomic Staff Projections released in March, June, September, and December), sets official policy interest rates and balance-sheet parameters for the 20-nation euro area.","points":[["How to read it","Check quarterly Staff Projections (Mar/Jun/Sep/Dec): inspect revisions to euro-area headline HICP inflation, core inflation, and GDP growth forecasts across the next two years."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission to U.S. equities and crypto operates through two competing channels: first, the global bond-yield channel—ECB easing pulls German Bund yields lower, which can pull U.S. Treasury yields down via cross-market bond arbitrage (supportive for risk valuations); second, the FX channel—because the euro makes up 57.6% of the U.S. Dollar Index (DXY), a dovish ECB that widens the U.S.–euro rate gap often weakens EUR/USD and mechanically boosts the DXY, creating a headwind for dollar-denominated commodities, U.S. multinational earnings translation, and crypto."],["Common mistake","Mixing up the three ECB rates (DFR, MRO, Marginal Lending) on economic calendars, or misreading technical operational framework adjustments to the MRO-DFR spread as a policy shock."]]},"i-68":{"title":"Bank of England MPC Rate Decision","line":"The Bank of England MPC Rate Decision, announced eight times a year by the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) on scheduled Thursdays at 12:00 noon London time alongside the Monetary Policy Summary and Minutes (plus the quarterly Monetary Policy Report and press conference in February, May, August, and November), sets the official Bank Rate for the United Kingdom.","points":[["How to read it","Compare the vote split against consensus: economic calendars forecast the exact vote split (e.g., expected 0–7–2 vs. actual 0–5–4); count how many members shifted camps."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission to U.S. equities and crypto is indirect: MPC rate moves, vote splits, and quarterly MPR forecasts → directly move UK government bond (Gilt) yields and the British pound (GBP/USD, where sterling accounts for 11.9% of the U.S. Dollar Index DXY) → spill over into global sovereign bond markets (including U.S. Treasuries) and multinational currency translation → filter into global risk appetite. While usually secondary to the Fed for U.S. stocks and crypto, sharp UK bond-market swings (such as the autumn 2022 gilt/LDI turmoil) can trigger cross-market volatility."],["Common mistake","Looking only at 'Bank Rate unchanged' and closing the page while missing a major shift in the 9-member vote split (e.g., from 8–1 to 5–4)."]]},"i-69":{"title":"Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Decision","line":"The Bank of Japan (BOJ) Monetary Policy Decision, announced eight times a year by the BOJ Policy Board at the conclusion of its two-day meeting (typically between 11:30 a.m. and 1:00 p.m.","points":[["How to read it","Read the overnight call rate guideline and JGB plan: check the basis-point change in the policy rate, the 9-member vote split, and any updates to monthly JGB purchases."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission to global markets runs through the Yen Carry Trade: an unexpected BOJ rate hike, faster JGB tapering, or hawkish guidance → lifts JGB yields and narrows the U.S.–Japan yield gap → drives rapid yen appreciation (a sharp drop in USD/JPY) → inflicts immediate FX losses and margin calls on leveraged funds that borrowed yen to hold dollar risk assets → forces cross-asset position unwinding that can drag down U.S. equity futures and crypto (as seen in early August 2024)."],["Common mistake","Reading the USD/JPY quote backward: a drop in USD/JPY from 150 to 135 means the yen strengthened (fewer yen per dollar), which squeezes yen borrowers; a rise from 150 to 160 means the yen weakened."]]},"i-70":{"title":"EIA Weekly Crude Oil Inventories","line":"EIA Weekly Crude Oil Inventories, published every Wednesday at 10:30 a.m.","points":[["How to read it","Compare actual against consensus (and Tuesday's API): note the million-barrel surprise relative to analyst forecasts."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission to broad equity and crypto markets runs through inflation and growth expectations: surprises in crude and product inventories → move WTI and Brent crude oil prices → directly impact Energy sector earnings while simultaneously moving retail gasoline prices, headline CPI inflation expectations, and consumer discretionary budgets → influence Treasury yields and Federal Reserve rate-cut expectations → filter into broader equities and crypto."],["Common mistake","Trading the single Commercial Crude headline line while ignoring offsetting builds in Gasoline and Distillate stocks or changes in refinery utilization."]]},"i-71":{"title":"EIA Weekly Natural Gas Storage","line":"The EIA Weekly Natural Gas Storage Report, published every Thursday at 10:30 a.m.","points":[["How to read it","Compare the Net Change (Bcf) against consensus: check how many Bcf the weekly injection or withdrawal beat or missed analyst forecasts."],["How it reaches markets","Transmission to broad equity and crypto markets is indirect and localized: weather swings (summer air-conditioning power burn and winter heating), LNG export flows, and dry-gas production → drive weekly storage injections or withdrawals → move Henry Hub natural gas futures and input costs for utilities, power generators, and chemical manufacturers → filter into household utility bills and the energy services component of CPI → marginally influence inflation expectations."],["Common mistake","Seeing a negative number like '-150 Bcf' in January and thinking inventories crashed unexpectedly—withdrawals are normal all winter, and if consensus expected a -180 Bcf draw, a -150 Bcf draw actually means 30 Bcf LESS gas was used than expected (bearish for gas prices)."]]},"i-72":{"title":"U.S. Treasury Yields and the Yield Curve","line":"U.S. Treasury yields act as the gravitational benchmark for global asset pricing. Bond prices and yields move inversely: when buyers bid up Treasury prices, yields fall; when sellers dump bonds, yields rise. Tracking the 2-year and 10-year yields and the curve shape is essential for reading macro liquidity.","points":[["How to read it","Calculate the 10Y-2Y spread: subtract the 2-Year yield from the 10-Year yield; positive values indicate a normal upward slope, while negative values indicate inversion."],["How it reaches markets","The 10-Year Treasury yield serves as the global risk-free discount rate denominator."],["Common mistake","Treating a yield curve inversion as an immediate stock-market short signal: lead times between inversion and historical recessions have varied widely from 6 to 24 months, with occasional false positives."]]},"i-73":{"title":"U.S. Dollar Index (DXY)","line":"The U.S. Dollar Index (DXY) measures the strength of the U.S. dollar against a basket of six major foreign currencies. Because global commodities, cross-border debt, and major crypto stablecoins are denominated in USD, DXY shifts reflect global dollar liquidity conditions.","points":[["How to read it","Cross-check the Fed Broad Dollar Index (DTWEXBGS): when analyzing Asian or emerging markets, supplement DXY with the trade-weighted Broad Index that includes CNY and MXN."],["How it reaches markets","First, denominator effect: USD-priced commodities (gold, oil) and crypto become more expensive for non-U.S. buyers when DXY rises."],["Common mistake","Assuming DXY measures the dollar against all world currencies: DXY excludes the Chinese Yuan, Mexican Peso, and Australian Dollar, acting largely as an inverse Euro index."]]},"i-74":{"title":"Cboe Volatility Index (VIX)","line":"Often dubbed the 'Fear Gauge', the VIX is not an opinion poll—it is a mathematical index derived directly from real-time S&P 500 option prices measuring expected 30-day volatility.","points":[["How to read it","Contextualize the regime: readings of 12–20 reflect calm option pricing, whereas spikes above 30 signal acute hedging demand; however, low VIX merely means cheap options, not zero risk."],["How it reaches markets","Because institutional portfolios are heavily net-long equities, sudden market selloffs prompt urgent buying of SPX put options for downside protection, bidding up option premiums and sending the VIX sharply higher."],["Common mistake","Attempting to buy and hold VIX ETFs long-term waiting for a crash: you cannot buy spot VIX, and VIX futures ETPs suffer relentless contango roll losses during calm markets."]]},"i-75":{"title":"Credit Spreads (High-Yield OAS)","line":"Credit spreads represent the extra default insurance premium that corporate borrowers must pay above risk-free U.S. Treasury yields. Because bondholders prioritize principal survival over upside narratives, credit spreads serve as a vital reality check on equity market rallies.","points":[["How to read it","Decompose the two legs: check whether spreads widened because corporate yields surged (credit selling) or because Treasury yields plunged (safe-haven buying)."],["How it reaches markets","While equity traders focus on growth stories, corporate bondholders focus on cash flow solvency."],["Common mistake","Mistaking a rising ('widening') credit spread for a bullish signal: like the VIX, credit spreads are an inverted stress gauge—higher numbers mean higher default risk and tighter liquidity."]]},"i-76":{"title":"China Consumer Price Index (CPI)","line":"China's Consumer Price Index (CPI) measures retail price changes across a basket of goods and services purchased by urban and rural households.","points":[["How to read it","Focus on Core CPI and Services CPI: stripping out food and energy reveals whether underlying household consumption and service demand are genuinely expanding."],["How it reaches markets","CPI gauges domestic consumption health and monetary policy room."],["Common mistake","Overreacting to wild YoY swings in January or February: Lunar New Year timing shifts between Jan and Feb create massive base-effect distortions that reverse the following month."]]},"i-77":{"title":"China Official Manufacturing PMI","line":"China's Official Manufacturing PMI is the earliest monthly macroeconomic indicator, released on the very last day of the current month.","points":[["How to read it","Compare New Orders against Production: if Production exceeds 52% while New Orders sit below 49%, factories are producing faster than demand, risking inventory gluts."],["How it reaches markets","Because hard data like Industrial Production and retail sales are not released until mid-next month, the month-end PMI sets the immediate market tone for China's growth momentum."],["Common mistake","Assuming divergence between the Official PMI and Caixin PMI means one is fabricated: their sample sizes (3,200 vs. 650), enterprise scales (large SOEs vs. small private exporters), and geographic weights differ by design."]]},"i-78":{"title":"Total Social Financing (TSF)","line":"Looking only at bank loans misses massive funding channels like government bonds, corporate bonds, and equity issuance.","points":[["How to read it","Inspect credit quality composition: check how much came from 'Corporate Medium-to-Long-Term Loans' (real capex) and 'Household Medium-to-Long-Term Loans' (mortgages) versus 'Short-Term Bill Financing' (used by banks to artificially pad loan quotas)."],["How it reaches markets","China's macro cycle is heavily credit-driven, making TSF stock YoY growth and loan composition key barometers for Chinese equities, bonds, and industrial commodities."],["Common mistake","Comparing monthly TSF flows sequentially (MoM): Chinese credit follows a rigid seasonal quota rhythm (huge in Jan/Mar/Jun, small in Apr/Jul/Oct); sequential comparisons are meaningless without YoY benchmarking."]]},"i-79":{"title":"Loan Prime Rate (LPR)","line":"When headlines report an 'interest rate cut' in China, they may refer to PBOC wholesale policy rates (like the 7-Day Reverse Repo) or the retail lending benchmark—the Loan Prime Rate (LPR).","points":[["How to read it","Analyze asymmetric cuts (1Y vs. 5Y+): a larger cut in the Over-5-Year LPR relative to the 1-Year LPR signals targeted support for housing mortgages and long-term infrastructure."],["How it reaches markets","LPR directly dictates debt-servicing costs for businesses and homeowners."],["Common mistake","Expecting existing monthly mortgage payments to drop the very next day after a 5Y+ LPR cut: existing floating-rate mortgages only update to the latest LPR on their contractual 'repricing date' (typically every 3, 6, or 12 months)."]]}};
